01 17592186045585 false] [17592186101128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268415 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101128 1071 "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(4 + x)^{4}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.05)^{4}$. Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186101128 1071 "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(4+x)^{4}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.05)^{4}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101128 1074 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=4,$ $b=x,$ and $n=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(4+x)}^{4} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(4)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(4)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(4)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(4)}^{4}+4{(4)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(4)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =256+4(64)(x)+6(16)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =256+256x+96{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n(4+x)^{4}≈256+256x+96{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.05$ into the expansion of $(4+x)^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4.05)^{4} & =(4+0.05)^{4} \\\\  & ≈256+256(0.05)+96(0.05)^{2} \\\\  & =256+256(0.05)+96(0.002\\,5) \\\\  & =256+12.8+0.24 \\\\  & =269.040,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098085 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186101130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268795 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101130 1071 "Find the entry $a_{32}$ of the matrix $A$, where\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186101130 1071 "Find the entry ${a}_{32}$ of the matrix $A$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101130 1074 "The entry ${a}_{32}$ lies at the intersection of the $3$rd row and $2$nd column. Therefore, ${a}_{32}=8.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098169 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186101144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268729 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101144 1071 "$(3 + x)^{5} =$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186101144 1071 "$(3+x)^{5}=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101144 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of $(3+x)^{5},$ we have  $a=3,$ $b=x,$ $n=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of $(3+x)^{5}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\frac{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\frac{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\frac{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+… \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+… \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098175 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186101145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268407 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101145 1071 "$(4-x)^{4}=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101145 1071 "What is $(4 - x)^{4}$?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186101145 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of $(4-x)^{4},$ we have  $a=4,$ $b=-x,$ $n=4.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of $(4-x)^{4}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(4-x)}^{4} & =(\\frac{4}{0}){(4)}^{4}{(-x)}^{0}+(\\frac{4}{1}){(4)}^{4-1}{(-x)}^{1}+(\\frac{4}{2}){(4)}^{4-2}{(-x)}^{2}+… \\\\  & =1{(4)}^{4}+4{(4)}^{3}{(-x)}^{1}+6{(4)}^{2}{(-x)}^{2}+… \\\\  & =256-256x+96{x}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098205 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186101150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268634 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101150 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int(2)/(x\\ln (2x))dx$." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186101150 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101150 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2x}\\cdot 2=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098211 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186101151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268409 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101151 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int(5)/(x\\ln^{2} (3x))dx$." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186101151 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{5}{x{ln}^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186101151 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3x}\\cdot 3=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{x{ln}^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{1}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {u}^{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186105714 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268895 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186105714 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268896 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186105714 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268901 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186105718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268911 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186105720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268914 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186105720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268915 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186105720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268916 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186110573 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268864 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186110573 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268865 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186115262 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269214 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186115262 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269215 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186115264 1038 #edb/r