2186045585 true] [17592186129326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269204 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126374 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186129484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268838 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129484 1071 "The initial velocity of a particle is $(5i - j)km/h$. Find the speed of the particle after $2$ hours if the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(i + 3j)km/h^{2}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186129484 1071 "The initial velocity of a particle is $(5i-j)\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Find the speed of the particle after $2$ hours if the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(i+3j)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129484 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu=5i-j,\\,a=i+3j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $2$ hours, the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(5i-j)+(i+3j)(2) \\\\  & =(5i-j)+(2i+6j) \\\\  & =(7i+5j)\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v| & =|7i+5j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{74}\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126380 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186129485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268621 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129485 1071 "A particle moves with constant acceleration. When $t = 0s$, the velocity of the particle is $(-2i - 8j)m/s$, and when $t = 4s$, the velocity of the particle is $2im/s$. Calculate the acceleration of the particle." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186129485 1071 "A particle moves with constant acceleration. When $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle is $(-2i-8j)\\,\\text{m/s},$ and when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle is $2i\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Calculate the acceleration of the particle." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129485 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=-2i-8j,\\,v=2i,\\,t=4,\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $a,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 2i & =(-2i-8j)+a(4) \\\\ 4i+8j & =4a \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{4}(4i+8j) \\\\  & =(i+2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126398 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186129488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268655 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129488 1071 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(i - 2j)m/s^{2}$. The velocity of the particle is $(2i - 5j)m/s$ when $t = 0s$. Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $(3i - j)m/s$." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186129488 1071 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(i-2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ The velocity of the particle is $(2i-5j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $(3i-j)\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129488 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=i-2j,\\,u=2i-5j,\\,v=3i-j\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can attempt to solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 3i-j & =(2i-5j)+(i-2j)t \\\\ i+4j & =(i-2j)t \\\\ i+4j & =ti-2tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=t \\\\ 4=-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has no solution." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126464 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186129499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268805 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129499 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = e^{t}, y = 4t, t ∈ [0, 2]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186129499 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=4t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186129499 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}4t\\sqrt{{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269142 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269143 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269144 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269145 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269146 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269147 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269255 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269256 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269257 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269258 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269260 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269139 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269140 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269141 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268859 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268860 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268861 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268868 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268873 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268874 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268875 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268869 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268877 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268878 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268879 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268881 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268882 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269253 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269261 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186133911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269262 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186134111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130792 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186134111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268804 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186134111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186134111 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $3a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186134111 1071 "${-x_{1} + 4x_{2} = 2; x_{1} - 4x_{2} = 2; 2x_{1} - 8x_{2} = 4$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2} ∈ R$\n\nwhat is the value of $3a + \\frac{b}{c}$?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186134111 1074 "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 96\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -32\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -24 & 8 \\\\ -24 & 96 & -32\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -24 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}+4{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}+4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n3a+\\frac{b}{c}=3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4}{1}=8.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269190 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269191 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [1759218