2186045585 true] [17592186152244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268964 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268965 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269193 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269194 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269195 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269196 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269197 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269198 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269199 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269200 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186148997 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268796 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152416 1071 "![](images/e4/e4b568fe2226154be401a2332c718f143954d98fdbd8a0c679fdb99fcdd47d14.png)\n\nSarah interviewed some parents, asking them how much time their children spend playing outdoors per day. The information she received is summarized in the scatter plot above.\n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. There is only one child who spends $3.5$ hours playing outdoors.\n2. There is only one child aged $6$ among those surveyed.\n3. None of the children spend less than $2$ hours playing outdoors." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152416 1071 "![](images/e4/e4b568fe2226154be401a2332c718f143954d98fdbd8a0c679fdb99fcdd47d14.png)\n\nSarah interviewed some parents, asking them how much time their children spend playing outdoors per day. The information she received is summarized in the scatter plot above. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There is only one child who spends $3.5$ hours playing outdoors.\n- There is only one child aged $6$ among those surveyed.\n- None of the children spend less than $2$ hours playing outdoors." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152416 1074 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $3.5,$ namely, $(5,3.5).$ This means that only one child spends $3.5$ hours playing outdoors.\n\n![](images/01/0103370189c7156f9677ca576e2480bc99314cd312702fca3ea3e50cb4f7287a.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $6,$ namely, $(6,2)$ and $(6,2.5).$ This means that there were two children of age $6$ among those surveyed.\n\n![](images/0a/0a8ca9628b48e643e1aa9e9f0216148881db237c2d29054b29f4377ad182503d.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, two children spend less than $2$ hours playing outdoors. Those children are represented by the two dots below the horizontal line.\n\n![](images/4d/4d9709c131045f2e440204f79ad3fd8ff22707312b0e99138da558393e8b7f6d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149015 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268850 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152419 1074 "The plot shows that the data points are scattered along a straight line.\n\n![](images/c8/c8597282ec934e535a01fa6f22be95b958d0393a5d0c5cd6358a9a7e620ba6a5.png)\n\nWe see that the line $q$ best fits the given data set." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149027 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268427 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152421 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,8)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(8,5).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-8}{8-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+b \\\\ 8 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(2)+b \\\\ 8 & =-1+b \\\\ 9 & =b \\\\ b & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+9.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149033 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268741 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152422 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,1)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(8,9).$\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-1}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149039 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268456 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152423 1071 "![](images/cb/cbe12f1271134a82701640386fcf89e39da4efbca3658569aff52cc743272660.png)\n\nEstimate the slope of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round your answer to the nearest $0.5$." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152423 1071 "![](images/cb/cbe12f1271134a82701640386fcf89e39da4efbca3658569aff52cc743272660.png)\n\nEstimate the slope of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round your answer to the nearest $0.5.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152423 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/05/0575edf80f4a6f26c8f1002404ac04331d4c5d1f81b644021bc6284a6212338a.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(2,2)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(6,8).$\n\nTherefore, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{6-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149045 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268426 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152424 1071 "![](images/e4/e4b948bc417deab7c83cb40f53ad4b55fbfb169fc4ec761a9365856a9457a8f5.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5$." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152424 1071 "![](images/e4/e4b948bc417deab7c83cb40f53ad4b55fbfb169fc4ec761a9365856a9457a8f5.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152424 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/fd/fd3ce3de75fa9296c721e3f3a28bef241eae6350819c4cc7a54707921c0c1fe4.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(2,7)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(8,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-7}{8-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{6} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 7 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 7 & =-1+b \\\\ 8 & =b \\\\ b & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+8.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149051 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268534 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152425 1071 "![](images/b2/b25aa3b36011df9f9a97ca55fce75b2bf53b6b35282d8d5896e348464d4bdd30.png)\n\nMr. Clark recorded the height and shoe size of the players on the basketball team he coaches. The information he collected is summarized in the scatter plot above.\n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Only one player has a shoe size of $9\\frac{1}{2}$.\n2. Only one player is $195cm$ tall.\n3. There are exactly two players taller than $185cm$." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152425 1071 "![](images/b2/b25aa3b36011df9f9a97ca55fce75b2bf53b6b35282d8d5896e348464d4bdd30.png)\n\nMr. Clark recorded the height and shoe size of the players on the basketball team he coaches. The information he collected is summarized in the scatter plot above. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Only one player has a shoe size of $9\\frac{1}{2}.$\n- Only one player is $195\\,\\text{cm}$ tall.\n- There are exactly two players taller than $185\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152425 1074 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is false. The scatter plot has two dots whose vertical coordinate equals $9.5,$ namely, $(180,9.5)$ and $(195,9.5).$ This means that two players have a shoe size of  $9\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2058bf02025ff359b509db01022c5d2e956d67243094a33692b90d6f1c2eac8.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement II is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $195,$ namely, $(195,\\,9.5).$ This means that only one of the players has a height of $195\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8b4364dc5de11eed12840bc644b1392b36160adfdf739b96f282c2367d999fa.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement III is false. According to the scatter diagram, three players have a height greater than  $185\\,\\text{cm},$ represented by the three dots to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/aa/aaf6d496c56c3cdc5a47e1c09e7640d7a3caa05446a75b705c042a56c116cb98.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149111 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268807 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152435 1071 "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152435 1071 "A body of mass $2kg$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t \\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) = 2t^{3} - 3t^{2} - 36t + 2$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152435 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-6t-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-6t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-3)(t+2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149117 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268499 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152436 1071 "A body of mass $1.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+t-5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186152436 1071 "A body of mass $1.5kg$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t \\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t) = 2t^{3} - 12t^{2} + t - 5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186152436 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+t-5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-24t+1) \\\\  & =12t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12t-24 & =0 \\\\ 12 & =24 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269224 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162015 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269227 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162015 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269228 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162015 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269229 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162015 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269230 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162016 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269231 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269157 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269158 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269159 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269160 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269161 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269162 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269163 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269164 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269236 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269242 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269243 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269245 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162104 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269250 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162104 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269251 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162104 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269252 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269246 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269247 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269248 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269249 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159368 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186162366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268625 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162366 1071 "A $2 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{3}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. Given that the eigenvalues of $A^{T}A$ are $5$, $3$, and $0$, find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186162366 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $5,$ $3,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162366 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=5\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159374 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186162367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268808 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162367 1071 "A $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{2}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. Given that the singular values of $A$ are $σ_{1} = 10$ and $σ_{2} = 2\\sqrt{5}$, find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186162367 1071 "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162367 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={10}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={10}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159386 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186162369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268555 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162369 1071 "![](images/f2/f26e876d5234313926a2cbaa8938e32c8a725cf3435ce3113361bfa3dc1b7997.png)\n\nA $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{2}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^{T}A$, which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $25$ of $A^{T}A$?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186162369 1071 "![](images/f2/f26e876d5234313926a2cbaa8938e32c8a725cf3435ce3113361bfa3dc1b7997.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $25$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186162369 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={7}^{2}=49,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={5}^{2}=25.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=25$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186166896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269165 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186166896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269166 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186166896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269167 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186166897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269168 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186166897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269170 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186166897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269171 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186166898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269172 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186166898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269173 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186166898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269174 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186166898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269176 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186166898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269177 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186166898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269178 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186167104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163672 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186167104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268658 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186167104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186167104 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(x) = 2x_{1}^{2} - 3x_{2}^{2} - x_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖ = 1$." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186167104 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186167104 1074 "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -3{x}_{1}^{2},\\,-{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge -3{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -3{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-3(1)=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -3.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-3.$ But before we conclude that $-3$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-3.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}-3(1)^{2}-(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-3.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186167106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163684 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186167106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268684 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186167106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186167106 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(x) = 7x_{1}^{2} + 8x_{2}^{2} + 5x_{3}^{2} - 4x_{2}x_{3}$ given that $‖x‖ = 1$." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186167106 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186167106 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )((8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,7,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=4.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186167107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163690 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186167107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268519 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186167107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186167107 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(x) = 5x_{1}^{2} + 3x_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖ = 1$." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186167107 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186167107 1074 "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n5{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge 3{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=3(1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge 3.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $3.$ But before we conclude that $3$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=3.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =5(0)+3(1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $3.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268975 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268976 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268978 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268979 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268980 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268982 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268984 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268985 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268987 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268991 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268992 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268993 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268994 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268995 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268997 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175951 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269226 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175954 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269184 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175972 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269020 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175972 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269021 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175972 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269022 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186175972 1038 #edb/r