7592186176066 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269129 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176067 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269130 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176067 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269131 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174238 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268752 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176335 1074 "The speed is $55$ miles per hour. To find the distance covered in $3$ hours, we multiply the speed by $3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\cdot \\text{time} \\\\  & =55\\times 3 \\\\  & =165.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the car covered $165$ miles in $3$ hours." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174244 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268672 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176336 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed in meters per minute, we divide the number of meters, $190,$ by the number of minutes, $2.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{190}{2} & =190÷2 \\\\  & =95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the person is $95$ meters per minute. \n\nTo find the distance covered in $5$ minutes, we multiply the speed by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\times \\text{time} \\\\  & =95\\times 5 \\\\  & =475.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the person can cover $475$ meters in $5$ minutes." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174250 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268543 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176337 1074 "The speed is $40$ kilometers per hour. To find the time it took the bike to cover $240$ kilometers, we divide the distance by $40.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{240}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{240÷10}{40÷10} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{4} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the bike was in motion for $6$ hours." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174262 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268451 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176339 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={4,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,5},\\,y={6,\\,3,\\,12,\\,11}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=2$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=8.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176339 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n$x = {4,-1, 0, 5}, y = {6, 3, 12, 11}$\nYou're given that $x^(―) = 2$ and $y^(―) = 8$. Calculate $Cov(x, y)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176339 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-2)(6-8)+(-1-2)(3-8)+(0-2)(12-8)+(5-2)(11-8)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2)(-2)+(-3)(-5)+(-2)(4)+(3)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-4+15-8+9] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174268 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268618 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176340 1071 "Calculate $Cov(x, y)$ for the following data sets:\n$x = {2, 3,-1, 4}, y = {4, 0, 2, 6}$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176340 1071 "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={2,\\,3,\\,-1,\\,4},\\,y={4,\\,0,\\,2,\\,6}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176340 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (2+3+(-1)+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (4+0+2+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2-2)(4-3)+(3-2)(0-3)+(-1-2)(2-3)+(4-2)(6-3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(0)(1)+(1)(-3)+(-3)(-1)+(2)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[0-3+3+6] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174340 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268777 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176352 1071 "If $\\cos a =-\\frac{1}{3}$, then $\\cos (-a) =$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176352 1071 "If $cos⁡a=-\\frac{1}{3},$ then $cos⁡(-a)=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176352 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-a) & =cos⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174346 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268709 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176353 1071 "If $\\tan a =-2$, then $\\tan (-a) =$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176353 1071 "If $tan⁡a=-2,$ then $tan⁡(-a)=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176353 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-a) & =-tan⁡a \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174358 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268471 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176355 1071 "If $\\operatorname{sec}⁡a=-3,$ then $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-a)=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186176355 1071 "If $\\sec a =-3$, then $\\sec (-a) =$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186176355 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph about the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-a) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡a \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186179659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268939 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186179659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268944 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186179659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268945 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186179659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268949 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186179660 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268940 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186179660 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268941 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186179661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268942 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186179661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268943 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186179661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268947 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186179661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268948 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178059 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186180070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268550 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180070 1071 "![](images/97/9749ca6ec771a94dc60c64a48c2b3924641db17ced56b131bc530e1eb51eb1a0.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the population of a town recorded at certain times between the years $2000$ and $2020$.\nUse the trend line to estimate in which year the town's population was $56000$." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186180070 1071 "![](images/97/9749ca6ec771a94dc60c64a48c2b3924641db17ced56b131bc530e1eb51eb1a0.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the population of a town recorded at certain times between the years $2000$ and $2020.$\nUse the trend line to estimate in which year the town's population was $56\\,000.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180070 1074 "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $y=56$ on the vertical axis.\n\n![](images/a9/a9d57651c5c342c3d619a20cfae2bc632df47f8924d49f400ed764870b7ae900.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/c8/c816d47fc84f31c3a436818777d9473b68e9d8468051c40fe9b7b94005cc89ee.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the horizontal axis.\n\n![](images/e6/e6cbfe34a6e3bd002171d2474542a12c66b7d88ce5989b0fbb4b3542f76a2155.png)\n\nTherefore, the population was $56\\,000$ in approximately the year $2000+10=2010.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178065 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186180071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268440 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180071 1071 "![](images/47/47b77320474a559f69e6ef08b8f6638aac920b05014820ab1b4b82690059c997.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y =-0.5x + 9$. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the value of $x$ when $y = 2$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186180071 1071 "![](images/47/47b77320474a559f69e6ef08b8f6638aac920b05014820ab1b4b82690059c997.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y=-0.5x+9.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the value of $x$ when $y=2.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180071 1074 "We can estimate the required value by substituting $y=2$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+9 \\\\ 2 & =-0.5x+9 \\\\ 2+0.5x & =9 \\\\ 0.5x & =7 \\\\ x & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $y=2$ we have $x≈14.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=2$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178071 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186180072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268670 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180072 1071 "![](images/e7/e7ca505879c9b628978a2be3fe2df4f3c248c2acc32e33a22cedbdf8cd4bd4da.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the population of a town $(y)$ recorded $x$ years after $2000$.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y = 0.1x + 53$. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the town's population in $2026$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186180072 1071 "![](images/e7/e7ca505879c9b628978a2be3fe2df4f3c248c2acc32e33a22cedbdf8cd4bd4da.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the population of a town $(y)$ recorded $x$ years after $2000.$\nThe equation of the trend line is $y=0.1x+53.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the town's population in $2026.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180072 1074 "First, notice that the year $2026$ is $26$ years since the year $2000.$\n\nTo predict the population in $2026,$ we need to substitute $x=26$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.1x+53 \\\\  & =0.1(26)+53 \\\\  & =2.6+53 \\\\  & =55.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, the population will be approximately $55\\,600$ in $2026.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=26$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178083 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186180074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268460 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180074 1071 "![](images/9f/9fed391b00d0159e58b2e760e0c68c5b0be656a440ae898a932bfec64469b5bb.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the population of a town recorded at certain times between the years $2000$ and $2020$.\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the year in which the population will be approximately $40000$.\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(2, 56)$ and $(18, 48)$.*\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186180074 1071 "![](images/9f/9fed391b00d0159e58b2e760e0c68c5b0be656a440ae898a932bfec64469b5bb.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the population of a town recorded at certain times between the years $2000$ and $2020.$\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the year in which the population will be approximately $40\\,000.$\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(2,56)$ and $(18,48).$*" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186180074 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/5b/5b2ab039994e7c2306cad7e30717fb136b5dfc3a028db284a0e95f49b56d580f.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,56)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(18,48).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{48-56}{18-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{16} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,56):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 56 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 56 & =-1+b \\\\ 57 & =b \\\\ b & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+57.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=40$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+57 \\\\ 40 & =-0.5x+57 \\\\ 0.5x+40 & =57 \\\\ 0.5x & =17 \\\\ x & =34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the population will be $40\\,000$ around the year $2000+34=2034.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=40$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269232 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269241 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269233 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269234 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269235 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269239 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269240 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184330 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269094 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269095 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269096 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269097 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269098 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269105 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269107 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269108 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184335 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269117 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184335 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269119 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184335 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269120 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184336 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269123 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184336 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269124 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184336 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269125 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184336 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269126 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184337 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269127 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184337 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269128 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269209 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186180991 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186184474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268770 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184474 1071 "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $(-i + j)mm$ with an acceleration of $(2i - 6j)mm/min^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(-3i + 2j)mm/min$. Find the position vector of the particle after $5$ minutes." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186184474 1071 "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $(-i+j)\\,\\text{mm}$ with an acceleration of $(2i-6j)\\,{\\text{mm/min}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(-3i+2j)\\,\\text{mm/min}.$ Find the position vector of the particle after $5$ minutes." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184474 1074 "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=-i+j,\\,u=-3i+2j,\\,a=2i-6j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=5\\,\\text{min}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(-i+j)+(-3i+2j)(5)+\\frac{1}{2}(2i-6j)(5)^{2} \\\\  & =(-i+j)+(-15i+10j)+25(i-3j) \\\\  & =(-16i+11j)+(25i-75j) \\\\  & =(9i-64j)\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186181009 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186184477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268803 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184477 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\ln 3}}5xe^{x^{2}}dx$." 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186184477 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}5x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186184477 1074 "Let's introduce the new variable $u={x}^{2}.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the new limits of integration:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}5x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}5{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}5{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(3-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(2) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186192790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268858 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186198448 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269182 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186198449 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269180 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186198565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186193991 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186198565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268614 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186198565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186198565 1071 "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{1}x_{2} + 4x_{2}^{2}$, when $‖x‖ = 1$, is equal to $5$. Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 false] [17592186198565 1071 "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $5.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186198565 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266157 1081 "$145$ miles" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266157 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266157 1084 #uuid "00100cbe-fb7e-5a60-bebf-70ac5833c23b" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266158 1081 "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266158 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266158 1084 #uuid "00508df2-67fc-5789-9478-c3e2d42771e8" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266159 1081 "{e}^{2}+2" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266159 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266159 1084 #uuid "007630bd-b544-59a4-967c-c0650fe766ee" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266160 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{5} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266160 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266160 1084 #uuid "009d9d37-4fe5-57ca-8373-86db8e14d805" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266161 1081 "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266161 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266161 1084 #uuid "015afdf6-11bd-580a-965b-b8d83ff6fdf3" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186266162 1081 "95{\\,}^{∘}C" 13194139533401 17592186045585 