855 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267641 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268855 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268855 1077 "selection" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268855 1079 #edb/ref 17592186267178 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268855 1080 #uuid "fba86cce-1c74-5dec-bd32-80e26d0db2bc" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268856 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266367 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268856 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266391 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268856 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266807 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268856 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267361 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268856 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267475 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268856 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268856 1077 "selection" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268856 1079 #edb/ref 17592186266807 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268856 1080 #uuid "fe6c15ab-cb59-5b2b-a36e-bc8f4709ba95" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268693 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268857 1069 "q-132210" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268857 1071 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,0)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟩.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268857 1072 #uuid "4a9b1874-ca32-5248-90d4-0970a7ef3359" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268857 1074 "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨1,0⟩,$ since the point $P(1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{({e}^{0}+1)}^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{({e}^{0}+1)}^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨1,0⟩+2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩+2⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩+⟨2,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268762 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268858 1069 "q-132253" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268858 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(3,-1,-2).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268858 1072 #uuid "1798a630-b3d2-5681-be69-eeaa1d664d97" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268858 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple and chain rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2})}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}{({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2})}^{-1/2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}) & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot (2x+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,-1,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(3,-1,-2)} & =\\frac{(3)(-1)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+4(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9+16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268687 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268859 1069 "q-132276" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268859 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268859 1072 #uuid "07938b36-7b86-5365-9a53-980eadb141d0" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268859 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x[\\,y\\,]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-2) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268697 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268860 1069 "q-132277" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268860 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268860 1072 #uuid "6f85a13d-0fd8-5d09-bfdb-9a5dcca1ba52" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268860 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{3x}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[\\,y\\,]_{0}^{3x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(3x-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1)^{4}}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268822 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268861 1069 "q-132282" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268861 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy\\,dx=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268861 1072 #uuid "63aade32-c436-5c43-9ff2-73d45e2de0f9" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268861 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4x\\,dx & ={[2{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268694 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268862 1069 "q-132287" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268862 1071 "![](images/d1/d185077fab32b6f2c85e53550da2f04f1128eb14610764ad131ed50d8bbc6ff0.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the line $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+2,$ and the lines $y=-x$ and $y=x,$ as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type I regions." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268862 1072 #uuid "c871cb0e-a4bd-51b2-b53f-123c0a5d7685" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268862 1074 "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/47/47eccc29cbbdd917a247dec2f10329f718ed133b7e8398312b4ee3bc6bbbd647.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,-x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+2}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-x}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268429 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268863 1069 "q-132292" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268863 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2,$ and the lines $y=x+2,$ $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ Express ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type I regions." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268863 1072 #uuid "30e25dca-92f8-5821-a1fd-77fa555fb29c" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268863 1074 "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/01/019f0f9f83ddf323d543d109677335cb3ae72a200baf834453a0eca16a9353ba.png)\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d2/d222e86f6af7c3ed2b35711114c9062d6f0c9b0f2551aa44cac33318c6e5813f.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,x+2\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\le y\\le x+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{x+2}^{{x}^{2}/2+2}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/2+2}^{x+2}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268410 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268864 1069 "q-132312" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268864 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-4,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-18<X<10)=0.1113,$ find  $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5080\\,$ | $\\,0.5120\\,$ | $\\,0.5160\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-18<X<10$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =-4.$*" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268864 1072 #uuid "41abce61-52a6-5108-9d32-3bdb65398fc2" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268864 1074 "First, note that the given interval is symmetric about the mean:\n\n$$\nP(-18<X<10)=P(-4-14<X<-4+14)\n$$\n\nSo, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-18<X<10) & =0.1113 \\\\ 2P(-4<X<10) & =0.1113 \\\\ P(-4<X<10) & =0.0557 \\\\ P(X<10)-P(X<-4) & =0.0557 \\\\ P(X<10)-0.5 & =0.0557 \\\\ P(X<10) & =0.5557 \\\\ P(X\\le 10) & =0.5557\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10) & =0.5557 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{10-(-4)}{\\sigma }) & =0.5557 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{14}{\\sigma }) & =0.5557 \\\\ \\frac{14}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5557)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.14)=0.5557\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.5557)=0.14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{\\sigma } & =0.14 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{14}{0.14} \\\\ \\sigma  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268446 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268865 1069 "q-132316" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268865 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{15}^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-5\\mu <X<7\\mu )=0.5762,$ determine $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-5\\mu <X<7\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$*" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268865 1072 #uuid "ecbc1ec9-99b3-5132-8a90-25b7b3f6bb9b" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268865 1074 "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -6\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu )=0.5762,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-5\\mu <X<7\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -6\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.5762 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.5762 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.2881 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +6\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.2881 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +6\\mu )-0.5 & =0.2881 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(X\\le \\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(X\\le 7\\mu ) & =0.7881\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7\\mu ) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{7\\mu -\\mu }{15}) & =0.7881 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{6\\mu }{15}) & =0.7881 \\\\ \\frac{6\\mu }{15} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7881) \\\\ \\frac{2\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.8)=0.7881\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)=0.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\mu }{5} & =0.8 \\\\ 2\\mu  & =4 \\\\ \\mu  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268830 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268866 1069 "q-132385" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268866 1071 "![](images/a4/a4515d2789a9b5c7a08210c0ef2611e7b6e661850b1fa822016b880427088bae.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2},$ and the lines $y=2-x$ and $y=x,$ as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268866 1072 #uuid "856a41b6-8856-5255-9b14-647a17c39faa" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268866 1074 "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=2-x & \\,⟹\\,x=2-y \\\\ y=x & \\,⟹\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/78/78aa61f11856dbf35287a0af016b56744fec354d6c4e64690a1a6fe466f164e8.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le y}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,-\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le 2-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y}}^{y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y}}^{2-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268706 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268867 1069 "q-132397" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268867 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y=-x,$ $y=6-4x$ and $y=2x.$ Express ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268867 1072 #uuid "6b2a0247-2a7d-59f9-9d65-c62df94a034f" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268867 1074 "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-x & \\,⟹\\,x=-y \\\\ y=6-4x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{y}{4} \\\\ y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/08/08237d1ca2e01d0cf620dfa06a4265d8053b7ee34d0b65036aad7d5734726e1c.png)\n\n \n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/c7/c756dc7924fc4c116601da907eb3b767b8a40647e063c146dad83b0ea660933b.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 0,\\,-y\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{y}{4}}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{y}{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-y}^{3/2-y/4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{3/2-y/4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268525 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268868 1069 "q-132402" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268868 1071 "![](images/bf/bfa591e51f42e8e52b67e61c23b02ea69b03bdecc1c4b815370ce6b2b0b5f213.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the $x$-axis and the curves $x={y}^{2}-4$ and $x=4-{y}^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268868 1072 #uuid "3cfac5a9-6edd-563f-8792-0eaf27928ce8" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268868 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/85/85d3faac9a27353a8ea5151b3a32f1065aecb02fd07e0b20727c35de0a7fb216.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}-4\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268542 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268869 1069 "q-132421" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268869 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dy.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268869 1072 #uuid "a5227312-f350-56e0-9d23-74214d0c95e0" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268869 1074 "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}[x]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dy & =[-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }-(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268846 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268870 1069 "q-132423" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268870 1071 "![](images/a4/a4265e8993b5c7ea5756fe55facd5313e5c2237cbdbc851195ebf458e5246060.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∬}_{D}2y\\,dA,$ where $D$ is the region shown in the picture above." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268870 1072 #uuid "c752feee-1897-55d8-b588-b76cf85bcde9" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268870 1074 "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/33/33a38da74b282f0ec1fb2fae9cb155feb2a5fd39128c7607b80c6b84fedd29b4.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,x-1\\le y\\le 3-x}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,3-x\\le y\\le x-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2y\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}2y\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}2y\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{x-1}^{3-x}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{3-x}^{x-1}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{x-1}^{3-x}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{3-x}^{x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[(3-x)^{2}-(x-1)^{2}]\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}[(x-1)^{2}-(3-x)^{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(8-4x)\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}(4x-8)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(8x-2{x}^{2}){|}_{1}^{2}+(2{x}^{2}-8x){|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =8(2-1)-2({2}^{2}-{1}^{2})+2({4}^{2}-{2}^{2})-8(4-2) \\\\  & =8-6+24-16 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268810 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268871 1069 "q-132464" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268871 1071 "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+6}?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268871 1072 #uuid "5d0ffa3b-787e-578b-bbef-1ac3a2b89d8c" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268871 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(x-2)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6)-(x-2)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+6-2{x}^{2}+4x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+4x+6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x-6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x-6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-10 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{10} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268698 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268872 1069 "q-132490" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268872 1071 "![](images/71/71f160891f01681b485b2eb88dccf63b05271fab8e24f069e3bab46f4168c454.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∬}_{D}6x\\,dA,$ where $D$ is the region shown in the picture above." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268872 1072 #uuid "147c6c88-ba8a-5b9e-bbac-4eb8f1f8b260" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268872 1074 "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb95d1507eb1cd527b6d6983cc0531d71c3852db002f188f220954a367de8d95.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-\\sqrt{y+2}\\le x\\le -\\sqrt{2-y}}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le y\\le 3,\\,-\\sqrt{y+2}\\le x\\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}6x\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}6x\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}6x\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+2}}^{-\\sqrt{2-y}}6x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+2}}^{0}6x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-\\sqrt{y+2}}^{-\\sqrt{2-y}}\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-\\sqrt{y+2}}^{0}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3[{(-\\sqrt{2-y})}^{2}-{(-\\sqrt{y+2})}^{2}]\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{3}3[{0}^{2}-{(-\\sqrt{y+2})}^{2}]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3(-2y)\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{3}3(-y-2)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{2}^{3}(y+2)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2}-3(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y){|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =-3(2)^{2}-3(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}+2(3))+3(\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+2(2)) \\\\  & =-12-\\frac{63}{2}+18 \\\\  & =-\\frac{51}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268570 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268873 1069 "q-132499" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268873 1071 "![](images/6d/6d6597bff5d28155e9a40f953b0ab0652f175e4d4d0aaf6f28c50e6d5bc907d2.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=y-1,\\,$ $x=5-\\frac{y}{2},$ and $y=1,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dA=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268873 1072 #uuid "b3df8379-3e6b-527e-8cdf-bcb2620eb7cc" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268873 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-\\frac{y}{2}+5 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}y & =6 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$1\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/e2/e2de1b62c6f3d0a15c0b95e3204f7889f6fc1ffbe108bdba7871c150091f6977.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,y-1\\le x\\le 5-\\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268445 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268874 1069 "q-132510" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268874 1071 "![](images/f3/f390e83f737b3b20931df5e97a9ea0750b8b190acba14d1cd963aaab5175ba50.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed between the curves $x=\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2$ and $x=2-{y}^{2},$ above the $x$-axis, as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,dA=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268874 1072 #uuid "87601be3-b1cb-5be0-9f64-84da32d08da7" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268874 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2 & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)+{y}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+4+2{y}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-4y-4 & =0 \\\\ (3y+2)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=0,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$,\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/1b/1b67c65c78dc847c2bf4bf53ad67f770a1406570548497edd55939c3a68a4c5c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268609 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268875 1069 "q-132512" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268875 1071 "![](images/d9/d90e750755a9520328e99583bfc80d9498ed34eb1695ba4ecd55b688d5b0216f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed between the curves $x={y}^{2}$ and $x=2-{y}^{2},$ above the $x$-axis, as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dA=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268875 1072 #uuid "78c81f5d-84e1-5433-9a31-94b0e49eda15" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268875 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=0,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/ee/eeadcddee3bc966372613ded9304c723768e9b81cd0299ad14084a94c9348463.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268876 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268700 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268876 1069 "q-132522" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268876 1071 "Change the order of integration in the double integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268876 1072 #uuid "6c252f64-4cd6-5786-9aa7-c27e4985b565" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268876 1074 "![](images/a6/a65b8ea83edc85a0b0ec3f3df6ee6f5bc841e9cb5d634aab963054f836965146.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+1\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y-1},\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=2.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 5,\\,\\sqrt{y-1}\\le x\\le 2},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d1/d10ba1db82f9d0366edb446f8bef564030fea2068066c7f05f388f0c9daa3414.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y-1}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268763 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268877 1069 "q-132527" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268877 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268877 1071 "Calculate ${∬}_{D}y\\,dA,$ where $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,2{y}^{2}\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+1}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268877 1072 #uuid "5c4cd592-9607-50f1-89a9-e38280b49bc5" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268877 1074 "The integral is defined over a type II region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,2{y}^{2}\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+1}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}y\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}y\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[\\,x\\,]_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y({y}^{2}+1-2{y}^{2})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268556 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268878 1069 "q-132529" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268878 1071 "Calculate ${∬}_{D}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dA,$ where $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-y\\le x\\le 2y}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268878 1072 #uuid "c2534dab-da9e-52d9-8510-a8b1311ad552" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268878 1074 "The integral is defined over a type II region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-y\\le x\\le 2y}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{2y}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{2y}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}[{\\int }_{-y}^{2y}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{-y}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{e}^{{y}^{3}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268545 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268879 1069 "q-132531" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268879 1071 "Calculate ${∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dA,$ where $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268879 1072 #uuid "2da71a28-356e-5389-9334-828f80b33c46" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268879 1074 "The integral is defined over a type II region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{6}-0)dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{5}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{5}\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{5}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{6}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{32}{3}-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{21}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268510 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268880 1069 "q-132559" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268880 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨s-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡s,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({s}^{2}+1)⟩,\\,s\\ge 0.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268880 1072 #uuid "bb73bb64-ede0-50a0-bba1-a62818a14994" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268880 1074 "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({s}^{2}+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{s}^{2}-1}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{{s}^{2}-1}{{s}^{2}+1}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4s}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\,\\frac{2(1-{s}^{2})}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟨4s,\\,2(1-{s}^{2})⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(4s)^{2}+{(2(1-{s}^{2}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{s}^{2}+4{s}^{4}-8{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4{s}^{4}+8{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4({s}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({s}^{2}+1)}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268765 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268881 1069 "q-132562" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268881 1071 "![](images/d5/d5f54020da277980a58151bf2a85911365e67ecae6832881e03ac8b9b6431e6a.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}4x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $16{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ above the $x$-axis, as shown in the picture." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268881 1072 #uuid "731d6d86-2944-5a6b-a46c-4a8ce3596432" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268881 1074 "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}} & =\\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}} \\\\ 1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our region lies above the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/d8/d8b8b05e9004f6b25f635e9c39421a8e8e27831a26a22273cf88572df89ffc45.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}4x\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,4x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,4x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,2x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,2{[\\,{x}^{2}\\,]}_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,2[4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16})]dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,[3-\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,(1-\\frac{1}{16}{y}^{2})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16})dy & =6{[y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{48}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =6(4-\\frac{64}{48}) \\\\  & =6(4-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268676 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268882 1069 "q-132563" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268882 1071 "![](images/e1/e15621dd6402a86de5c5d172880c7cbdc2a933e852259ad81d5ebcf9a89b9984.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}2x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10$ and $y=2x+1,$ above the line $y=1,$ as shown in the picture." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268882 1072 #uuid "d1086f62-dfd2-5e5a-af56-96fee344d402" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268882 1074 "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{10-{y}^{2}} \\\\ y=2x+1 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10-{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{10-{y}^{2}} & =y-1 \\\\ (2\\sqrt{10-{y}^{2}})^{2} & =(y-1)^{2} \\\\ 40-4{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2}-2y+1 \\\\ 5{y}^{2}-2y-39 & =0 \\\\ (5y+13)(y-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=1$, the $y$-limits are \n$1\\le y\\le 3.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/33/33487fff6ff0b0e74bab4e6d6acdcb6902d40e938525d7de92d684ed5d98273a.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)\\le x\\le \\sqrt{10-{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}2x\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{(y-1)/2}^{\\sqrt{10-{y}^{2}}}2x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{(y-1)/2}^{\\sqrt{10-{y}^{2}}}2x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[{\\int }_{(y-1)/2}^{\\sqrt{10-{y}^{2}}}2x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[{x}^{2}]_{(y-1)/2}^{\\sqrt{10-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(10-{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}(y-1)^{2})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(10-{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}(y-1)^{2})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(10-{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}(y-1)^{2})dy & ={[10y-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{12}(y-1)^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(30-9-\\frac{2}{3})-(10-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{3}-\\frac{29}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268455 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268883 1069 "q-132567" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268883 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268883 1072 #uuid "f888c82d-82ba-57ac-963b-02774f1c6aab" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268883 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268574 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268884 1069 "q-132569" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268884 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268884 1072 #uuid "3f56f917-a9a1-5b17-9bc1-0d6e9b568c67" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268884 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{7}{2})+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈1.292\\,50$ rounded to five decimal places\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x≈-1.849\\,10$ rounded to five decimal places\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx≈1.292\\,50,\\,x≈-1.849\\,10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-1.849\\,10$ and $x≈1.292\\,50.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}≈-1.849\\,10$ and ${x}_{2}≈1.292\\,50.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-1.849\\,10\\cdot 1.292\\,50≈-2.39\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268785 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268885 1069 "q-132574" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268885 1071 "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}-1}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268885 1072 #uuid "88491dc6-a6a9-5e2a-ab57-c63ec8866fd2" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268885 1074 "![](images/92/926e4c717707d13fdb9562e149d2f41c3ab657b42fbae35c0953019ab93a06b6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,-{y}^{2}-1\\le x\\le -y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](images/89/8933233e8aca4a36ee90ffec69ca6e93d0523d3fa50c400d2cdea78f9ec80812.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $y=y(x),$  are \n\n$\\,$ $y=\\sqrt{-x-1}\\,$ and $\\,y=1.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=-x.$ \n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le -1,\\,\\sqrt{-x-1}\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le -x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}-1}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{-x-1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268748 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268886 1069 "q-132577" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268886 1071 "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}/4}^{{y}^{2}/4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268886 1072 #uuid "bec8d182-a1f9-590a-9ca9-2e6ea3a0b21f" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268886 1074 "![](images/72/7212dd3ebd7ef05423c29ca6c3bd8524f8b336563d4618a960a375121c2b2157.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](images/3e/3eedf80b599bfaa97ad5cb42178359e24048938637f60a93e7ebb15cd8d8d2e4.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $y=y(x),$  are \n\n$\\,$ $y=2\\sqrt{-x}\\,$ and $\\,y=2.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=2\\sqrt{x}\\,$ and $\\,y=2.$ \n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,2\\sqrt{-x}\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}/4}^{{y}^{2}/4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{-x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268780 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268887 1069 "q-132579" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268887 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+xy$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268887 1072 #uuid "ea3a7950-5517-53eb-8054-3498346f46a1" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268887 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-1-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268774 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268888 1069 "q-132584" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268888 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(1,-3,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+y\\sqrt{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268888 1072 #uuid "09f5edfe-2ef7-5aa3-b9a3-3d13ae59dd49" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268888 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-3,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-3) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{1}^{2}(-3)^{2}}+(-3)\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}-3(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268535 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268889 1069 "q-132585" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268889 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Given that the point $P(4,-2,5)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y\\sqrt{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268889 1072 #uuid "484f8b36-97b4-5e4f-a875-d05f61c48bf6" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268889 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(4,-2,5)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(4,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)} & =(-2)\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =-2(2) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(4,-2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)}(x-4) \\\\ z-5 & =-4(x-4) \\\\ z & =-4x+21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(4,-2,5)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+21\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268827 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268890 1069 "q-132587" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268890 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2xy+2$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268890 1072 #uuid "1c434702-618c-5a71-8a4a-aadc6bd7fee6" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268890 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)(2)+2 \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =6(x-1) \\\\ z & =6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268482 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268891 1069 "q-132589" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268891 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268891 1072 #uuid "5c5dd738-1c94-548e-aa53-3b5d32dc4abc" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268891 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(e,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =1(y-2) \\\\ z & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(e,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268481 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268892 1069 "q-132590" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268892 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{xy}{\\pi }$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268892 1072 #uuid "90718d28-70a4-5587-a36e-fa57ec8d26d4" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268892 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(2)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}(x-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\ z-2\\pi  & =-2(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ z & =-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268841 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268893 1069 "q-132593" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268893 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268893 1072 #uuid "22a41e65-3d40-52de-a0eb-a4c7353c0c15" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268893 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z-0 & =4(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z & =4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268669 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268894 1069 "q-132595" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268894 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268894 1072 #uuid "ac300975-45c9-5a2c-a8b5-5d2addf48a83" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268894 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(\\sqrt{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},2) & =-\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{3}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268836 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268895 1069 "q-132596" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268895 1071 "![](images/d5/d51ed648be79858e9dd211494f706c1f8658364e10d14dca8418b69cf4ab99de.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=-2$ and $x=4.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268895 1072 #uuid "309c7c86-8c72-5949-9d8a-de8720f95227" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268895 1074 "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,-2) & -2 & (-2,4) & 4 & (4,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{no extr.} & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & + & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(-2,4).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268610 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268896 1069 "q-132598" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268896 1071 "![](images/3f/3f542d08fede8a5bad978b62bece12070b02e0d3b4369c804454356f07d0832a.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=-3$ and $x=2.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268896 1072 #uuid "14c35a9b-7f1f-551e-af4e-0a49890c9010" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268896 1074 "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,-3) & -3 & (-3,2) & 2 & (2,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{no extr.} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-3,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268637 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268897 1069 "q-132599" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268897 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y-1}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268897 1072 #uuid "7da12630-1fa6-595d-a465-5095a1603e7f" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268897 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,1) & ={e}^{1-1}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-1) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,1) & ={f}_{x}(2,1)(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,1,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=5x-6$ (or $5x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨5,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-5⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-5⟩=⟨1,0,5⟩$ is also parallel to it." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268820 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268898 1069 "q-132601" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268898 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268898 1072 #uuid "86de2721-fbfb-53fc-8404-fca3429d9ecc" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268898 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{y}(-2,2)(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-1$ (or $2y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j+2\\,k.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268737 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268899 1069 "q-132602" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268899 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,7)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268899 1072 #uuid "ecd11e65-1ebe-5f6c-b826-3c8a8ba99741" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268899 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-7 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,7)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+7$ (or $x-z+7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268753 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268900 1069 "q-132604" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268900 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268900 1072 #uuid "e644a49d-2ffc-52d7-91ba-f94a7b4d9023" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268900 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-j+k.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268540 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268901 1069 "q-132605" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268901 1071 "![](images/79/799fd5d6528a9a66060d47e8f7613643ee5a305ed0dc494fabc1fc0ecf089e84.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. The points $A,B,$ and $C$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=-3,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $A,B,$ and $C,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268901 1072 #uuid "aac1e652-c0d9-5c3c-9830-b4ff3ad5df01" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268901 1074 "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,-3) & -3 & (-3,0) & 0 & (0,3) & 3 & (3,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{no extr.} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & + & 0 & - & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268627 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268902 1069 "q-132635" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268902 1071 "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268902 1072 #uuid "71ac5e06-9913-52a8-814a-fe270e8af8f7" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268902 1074 "![](images/8e/8ed09966d887f66f36402df0047ba02444d92b8aa4c3cb744721bb4ad6278812.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le x\\le 0}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=-1.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le {x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f7/f707a71f68f2e030696029d9a919e16ae6afda18a1d60dc3f8113f03143243e7.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)[{\\int }_{-1}^{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)\\cdot y\\,{|}_{-1}^{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{4}+4(0))-(-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{4}+4(-1))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3-1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268478 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268903 1069 "q-132638" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268903 1071 "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}4x{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268903 1072 #uuid "2241615f-0c50-5601-a5f3-153a8d9e79c8" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268903 1074 "![](images/26/262e1b0c4e0ddf338c527b487fcadc85baac12712f91cb4c54b771e49add25b9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/48/48c874b069b55599f76a8fe1aa50c5f4db36ff8943d264a107452668d689174b.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}4x{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-y}}4x{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-y}}4x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2(4-y){e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & ={e}^{8(4)-{4}^{2}}-{e}^{8(0)-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268597 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268904 1069 "q-132640" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268904 1071 "${\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{x+2}{e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268904 1072 #uuid "1062949f-9ed1-5794-826b-191a247be0ce" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268904 1074 "![](images/3d/3d51eade27c19494ac30ffdaf5baf8d30ffc9d18dcc3e1186cbc59412fe000c7.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le x+2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x+2\\,⟹\\,x=y-2,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y-2\\le x\\le 0},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/39/39072bd4c2d4bd763186f4d86921eaacb990df1e862f1c60b1f764ccff422e98.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{x+2}{e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-2}^{0}{e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}[{\\int }_{y-2}^{0}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}\\cdot x\\,{|}_{y-2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-y){e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2(2)-{2}^{2}/2}-{e}^{2(0)-{0}^{2}/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268600 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268905 1069 "q-132643" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268905 1071 "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268905 1072 #uuid "a263e5dc-ce78-5c3c-8cfc-58a5ae184325" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268905 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{(x-y)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268686 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268906 1069 "q-132644" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268906 1071 "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{-x-3}^{1}6x{e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268906 1072 #uuid "3a5ddf06-75ff-5dae-b8e8-183a3beaa17d" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268906 1074 "![](images/bb/bb17a906727b436562060f84cb1acae42944587cdec6311627e30163485a72d9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le x\\le -1,\\,-x-3\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=-x-3\\,⟹\\,x=-y-3,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=-1.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 1,\\,-y-3\\le x\\le -1},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f8/f80d088bb764b5dd6aa1c6d26ebd7a5b2a3894c072d81966dec4975fc0e08e59.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{-x-3}^{1}6x{e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{-y-3}^{-1}6x{e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}[{\\int }_{-y-3}^{-1}6x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-y-3}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}3((-1)^{2}-(-y-3)^{2}){e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}3(1-(y+3)^{2}){e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{3(1)-(1+3)^{3}}-{e}^{3(-2)-(-2+3)^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-61}-{e}^{-7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268666 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268907 1069 "q-132645" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268907 1071 "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)}{y},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ exists." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268907 1072 #uuid "42374463-e974-5c5f-93ed-37845f5f27ea" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268907 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & >0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y(x+z)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & \\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268596 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268908 1069 "q-132649" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268908 1071 "Given that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{{e}^{x/y}}{\\sqrt{y}},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{(y-2x)\\,{e}^{x/y}}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268908 1072 #uuid "a66c3ca8-3dc1-5636-a0ae-fd45747b8bcc" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268908 1074 "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x/y}}{\\sqrt{y}}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268565 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268909 1069 "q-132650" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268909 1071 "Given that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂z}=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268909 1072 #uuid "f03ff15b-864a-54f0-95c4-85eaad4622ab" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268909 1074 "First, notice that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{z}(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} & ={D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268645 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268910 1069 "q-132651" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268910 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268910 1072 #uuid "9c09835a-efb6-5fd7-a6fa-37a21ebe5b95" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268910 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268584 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268911 1069 "q-132671" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268911 1071 "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-27{x}^{2}-30x+9$ is strictly decreasing." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268911 1072 #uuid "d4df76f2-3dd2-5905-a44d-dc1adb625816" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268911 1074 "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-54x-30 \\\\  & =6(2{x}^{2}-9x-5),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6(2{x}^{2}-9x-5)<0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6(2x+1)(x-5)<0,\n$$\n\nand the graph of $y=6(2x+1)(x-5)$ has roots at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/7e/7eec3fc84b851b3b39e5ae8ecff1343f39393d1dd8f7a6cce8e8904dc1c65d48.png)\n\nThe graph is negative on the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},5)$ between the roots, so we conclude that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ on the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},5).$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},5).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268433 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268912 1069 "q-132675" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268912 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $y$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(1,-1,1)$ is defined" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268912 1072 #uuid "72f1f14f-947a-5dd6-902d-418c94ea31fa" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268912 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,0)=0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-1)\\cdot (-1))=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-1)}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,-1,1)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,-1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot (-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268744 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268913 1069 "q-132680" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268913 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,2)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268913 1072 #uuid "9dc50a8a-45d6-510c-8209-df3404b44621" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268913 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(4,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(4,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(4,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,2) & =\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {f}_{y}(4,2) & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,2)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,2)=\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{2}}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,2)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{3}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268848 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268914 1069 "q-132682" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268914 1071 "![](images/0b/0ba11dcbffa05bb78ee8d9bdd6ca8618c789cf13ab208f17d169a7f700cf9e3e.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268914 1072 #uuid "d46c908a-2c99-571a-8985-acae6c5a3064" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268914 1074 "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(-2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(2)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=5$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(5)<0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(2).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268494 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268915 1069 "q-132687" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268915 1071 "![](images/37/37c80a68fc1ee7dd534c795a7cfae13e44f63b09df4f2e948b29acbd1fb7ff06.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268915 1072 #uuid "b430ee23-dba0-5eae-88e0-96ab31b53b2a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268915 1074 "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-4$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(-4)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-3$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(-3)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(-1)<0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-4).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268730 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268916 1069 "q-132691" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268916 1071 "![](images/e0/e0bd848039c3f2db6dd298af47eb907035c47c0c22c1cc72afaaa233f29a0195.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268916 1072 #uuid "c8ce82eb-214b-50bc-8cb1-8727b868ae6e" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268916 1074 "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(-1)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(2)>0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(2).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268842 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268917 1069 "q-132712" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268917 1071 "In a factory, the amount of sugar on a cookie is normally distributed with a mean of $15$ grams and a standard deviation of $0.8$ grams. What is the probability that the amount of sugar on a randomly selected cookie is less than $14$ grams? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ | $\\,0.1075\\,$ | $\\,0.1056\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ | $\\,0.1271\\,$ | $\\,0.1251\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1515\\,$ | $\\,0.1492\\,$ | $\\,0.1469\\,$ |" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268917 1072 #uuid "0ab1b6ae-ab3a-526a-95c6-2717bb1334a7" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268917 1074 "Let $X$ represent the amount of sugar (in grams) on the cookies. Then we have $X∼N(15,{0.8}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X<14).$ First, we write this as a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\nP(X<14)=P(X\\le 14)\n$$\n\nThen, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 14) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{14-15}{0.8}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.25)=0.1056≈0.11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the amount of sugar on a randomly selected cookie is less than $14$ grams is approximately $0.11.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268718 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268918 1069 "q-132713" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268918 1071 "Find $B=\\overset{―}{A}$, where\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3-2\\text{i} & 4\\text{i} \\\\ -1-3\\text{i} & 5 & 3-5\\text{i} \\\\ 2+\\text{i} & -5-\\text{i} & -3+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].$ What is the value of ${b}_{31}+{b}_{32}?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268918 1072 #uuid "0650a246-400c-5ccd-af24-2667ccbb4784" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268918 1074 "Taking the complex conjugate of each entry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{A} & =\\overset{―}{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3-2\\text{i} & 4\\text{i} \\\\ -1-3\\text{i} & 5 & 3-5\\text{i} \\\\ 2+\\text{i} & -5-\\text{i} & -3+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{-4} & \\overset{―}{3-2\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{4\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{-1-3\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{5} & \\overset{―}{3-5\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{2+\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{-5-\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{-3+3\\text{i}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3+2\\text{i} & -4\\text{i} \\\\ -1+3\\text{i} & 5 & 3+5\\text{i} \\\\ 2-\\text{i} & -5+\\text{i} & -3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${b}_{31}+{b}_{32}=(2-\\text{i})+(-5+\\text{i})=-3.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268605 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268919 1069 "q-132715" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268919 1071 "Find $B=\\overset{―}{A}$, where\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} & -2+2\\text{i} & 5-5\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i} & -1+5\\text{i} & -3+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5-3\\text{i} & 4-4\\text{i} & 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].$ What is the value of ${b}_{12}+{b}_{23}?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268919 1072 #uuid "b1e3099e-fc04-5e9b-8698-7f21fd8d4845" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268919 1074 "Taking the complex conjugate of each entry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{A} & =\\overset{―}{[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} & -2+2\\text{i} & 5-5\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i} & -1+5\\text{i} & -3+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5-3\\text{i} & 4-4\\text{i} & 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{3\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{-2+2\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{5-5\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{-1+5\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{-3+4\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{-5-3\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{4-4\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{4\\text{i}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} & -2-2\\text{i} & 5+5\\text{i} \\\\ -\\text{i} & -1-5\\text{i} & -3-4\\text{i} \\\\ -5+3\\text{i} & 4+4\\text{i} & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${b}_{12}+{b}_{23}=(-2-2\\text{i})+(-3-4\\text{i})=-5-6\\text{i}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268590 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268920 1069 "q-132728" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268920 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2)?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$\n- ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268920 1072 #uuid "a68d3690-fbcf-53d1-acbf-a5134efed5cb" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268920 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268736 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268921 1069 "q-132764" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268921 1071 "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}$ given that $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268921 1072 #uuid "9ff84a14-2b18-5ea3-8551-3c53774a8d4e" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268921 1074 "To find $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268922 1069 "q-132800" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268922 1071 "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}$ given that $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x+y}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268922 1072 #uuid "12894c28-d9fc-5b1a-9ce3-909e531bc682" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268922 1074 "To find $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x+y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{(x+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268580 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268923 1069 "q-132860" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268923 1071 "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$0.75.$ Additionally, in $94%$ of cases the admission fee is between $$0.7\\mu $ and $$1.3\\mu .$ Find the percentage of cases when the admission fee is lower than $$5.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268923 1072 #uuid "20ed4d5f-dc29-565d-bad0-638efeb4a044" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268923 1074 "Let $X$ represent a randomly selected admission fee. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.75}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<5).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.7\\mu <X<1.3\\mu )=0.94.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.47 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.5+0.47 \\\\ P(X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.97 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75}) & =0.97 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75}) & =0.97 \\\\ \\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97) \\\\ 0.4\\mu  & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.97)≈1.8808.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.4\\mu  & =1.8808 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.8808}{0.4} \\\\  & ≈4.702.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(Z<\\frac{5-4.702.}{0.75}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.40) \\\\  & =Φ(0.40) \\\\  & =0.6554 \\\\  & ≈65.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately in $65.5%$ of cases, the admission fee is lower than $$5.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268843 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268924 1069 "q-132875" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268924 1071 "Find ${f}_{xx}(x,y)+{f}_{yy}(x,y)$ given that  ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268924 1072 #uuid "aff3c98c-3437-5c9a-af97-e9221f961ad1" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268924 1074 "To find ${f}_{xx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}_{yy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y)+{f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268504 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268925 1069 "q-132891" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268925 1071 "Find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)\\cdot {g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(0,1)$ given that ${g}_{x}(x,y)=2{e}^{2x-y}$ and ${g}_{y}(x,y)=-{e}^{2x-y}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268925 1072 #uuid "607d6de3-387d-52a2-8e01-8c94e83518ce" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268925 1074 "To find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{2x-y}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{2x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({g}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{2x-y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we evaluate ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ and ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(0,1) & =4{e}^{2(0)-1}=4{e}^{-1} \\\\ {g}_{yy}(0,1) & ={e}^{2(0)-1}={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{xx}(0,1)\\cdot {g}_{yy}(0,1)=4{e}^{-1}\\cdot {e}^{-1}=4{e}^{-2}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268424 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268926 1069 "q-132894" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268926 1071 "Find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)-{g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ given that ${g}_{x}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ and ${g}_{y}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268926 1072 #uuid "3aaf83dc-c135-54d5-b395-8dc2f637ad0a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268926 1074 "To find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({g}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we evaluate ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ and ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ {g}_{yy}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{xx}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})-{g}_{yy}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268742 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268927 1069 "q-132897" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268927 1071 "Find ${g}_{xy}(x,y)+{g}_{yx}(x,y)$ at $(-1,2)$ given that ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x+2y}$ and ${g}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2}{x+2y}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268927 1072 #uuid "619c15a9-a007-5612-9fb8-0f8afb4a8030" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268927 1074 "To find ${g}_{xy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{x+2y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(x+2y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find ${g}_{yx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({g}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{2}{x+2y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(x+2y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we evaluate ${g}_{xy}(x,y)$ and ${g}_{yx}(x,y)$ at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xy}(-1,2) & =-\\frac{2}{((-1)+2(2))^{2}}=-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ {g}_{yx}(-1,2) & =-\\frac{2}{((-1)+2(2))^{2}}=-\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{xy}(-1,2)+{g}_{yx}(-1,2)=-\\frac{4}{9}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268560 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268928 1069 "q-132940" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268928 1071 "Evaluate ${f}_{xyz}(-5,2,3)$ for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+z).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268928 1072 #uuid "e5449d51-c794-5d29-87ea-e1688a095f9f" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268928 1074 "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+z)) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{xy}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+z)) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{y+z} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{xyz}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{xy}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyz}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({f}_{xy}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{2x}{y+z}) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{y+z}) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{(y+z)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{(y+z)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate ${f}_{xyz}(x,y,z)$ at $(-5,2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyz}(-5,2,3) & =-\\frac{2(-5)}{(2+3)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268551 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268929 1069 "q-132961" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268929 1071 "Evaluate ${f}_{xxz}(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\pi )$ for $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268929 1072 #uuid "01b2f238-4be0-502a-a439-500d85cd72b9" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268929 1074 "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡xyz) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\cdot yz \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{xx}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{x}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =yz\\cdot (-yz\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{xxz}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{xx}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xxz}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({f}_{xx}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =-{y}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =-{y}^{2}\\cdot (2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)+xy{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =-{y}^{2}z(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate ${f}_{xxz}(x,y,z)$ at $(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xxz}(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ) & =-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi )+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268757 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268930 1069 "q-132984" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268930 1071 "Evaluate ${f}_{xyz}(-1,2,1)$ for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268930 1072 #uuid "5eef046b-e675-5de1-b784-361653847836" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268930 1074 "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{xy}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{xyz}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{xy}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyz}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({f}_{xy}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate ${f}_{xyz}(x,y,z)$ at $(-1,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyz}(-1,2,1) & =12(-1)^{2}(2)(1)=24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268421 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268931 1069 "q-133053" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268931 1071 "The diameter of the kibble of a particular cat food brand is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ and a standard deviation of $0.7\\,\\text{mm}.$ Additionally, the diameter of $20%$ of kibbles is less than $4.4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter. Find the percentage of kibbles whose diameter is less than $4\\,\\text{mm},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0808\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0968\\,$ | $\\,0.0951\\,$ | $\\,0.0934\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1151\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ |" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268931 1072 #uuid "763a853d-691e-5772-a9d4-a4d4d0b3eb60" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268931 1074 "Let $X$ represent the diameter of a randomly selected kibble. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the $P(X<4).$\n\nWe know that $P(X<4.4)=0.20.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4.4) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)≈-0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & =-0.8416 \\\\ 4.4-\\mu  & =-0.58912 \\\\ \\mu  & =4.98912\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{4-4.98912}{0.7}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.41) \\\\  & =0.0793 \\\\  & ≈7.9%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $7.9%$ of kibbles are less than $4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268714 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268932 1069 "q-133250" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268932 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=8xy-5{x}^{3}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=6x{y}^{3}$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268932 1072 #uuid "53d0afb1-1358-5254-8618-617a2b1cbc7d" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268932 1074 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2})=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy)=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y)=8y-15{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-5{x}^{3})=8y-15{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=18{x}^{2}y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{3})=6{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268933 1069 "q-133331" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268933 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{e}^{y+1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y+1}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=7x-3y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-7y$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268933 1072 #uuid "27d730e5-f1a8-51a8-a220-f1ae1c972c84" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268933 1074 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7x-3y)=-3 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-7y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268505 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268934 1069 "q-133371" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268934 1071 "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{xyx}(1,\\pi )$ if ${f}_{xx}(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268934 1072 #uuid "f5acd057-670a-5a23-995b-650a92790dc3" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268934 1074 "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyx}(x,y) & ={f}_{xxy}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{xx}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{e}^{xy}\\cdot (-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{e}^{xy}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi ,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{xyx}(1,\\pi )={1}^{3}{e}^{1\\cdot \\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi ))=-{e}^{\\pi }\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268702 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268935 1069 "q-133373" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268935 1071 "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ if ${f}_{xy}(x,y)=7+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268935 1072 #uuid "33686134-3f7d-5a05-9109-0d2ac79b776f" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268935 1074 "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{xyy}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxy}(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3})=6\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=3\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268557 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268936 1069 "q-133382" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268936 1071 "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x{e}^{yz}}{x+y},\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}=-\\frac{z{e}^{yz}}{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(1,1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂x∂y}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268936 1072 #uuid "9379a450-3ef7-54fa-b6cb-c35ea0f78eee" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268936 1074 "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nHence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x{e}^{yz}}{x+y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{yz}\\cdot (\\frac{1\\cdot (x+y)-x\\cdot 1}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{yz}}{(x+y)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-\\frac{z{e}^{yz}}{x+y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot ({e}^{yz}+yz{e}^{yz}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{yz}(1+yz)}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}}{(1+1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =-\\frac{{e}^{1\\cdot 0}(1+1\\cdot 0)}{1+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,1,0)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268679 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268937 1069 "q-133395" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268937 1071 "Evaluate ${f}_{yzx}(2,1,3)$ given that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n{f}_{xy}(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x-y+z}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268937 1072 #uuid "4cca6146-d9c7-5b1f-ad16-9d00e8365a81" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268937 1074 "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yzx}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{xyz}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({f}_{xz}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x-y+z}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-y+z}}-\\frac{1}{x+y+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point  $(2,1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yzx}(2,1,3) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2-1+3}}-\\frac{1}{2+1+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268791 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268938 1069 "q-133639" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268938 1071 "![](images/3e/3e0b0fc97e260decaa74a9a751b6422714d1ca7365aff3cc05e57f8904b9067a.png)\n\nA company produces cars. The scatter plot above shows the correspondence between the number of cars produced each week and the company's profit, in thousands of dollars. Which of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268938 1072 #uuid "0adfe027-b42e-59df-88ac-44f11f5426bf" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268938 1074 "The plot shows that the data points are scattered along a curve, not a straight line.\n\n![](images/e9/e960deb7d006d665abb97a83791204190af503f98dc3a23eae407e6c03e155df.png)\n\nTherefore, there is no linear trend in the data." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268832 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268939 1069 "q-133880" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268939 1071 "![](images/4d/4de50fc0b2b2f05b9ca60e28ffc65900a2b0780549fbf1cf32150a903a08faf5.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nUse the trend line to predict the value of $x$ when $y=20.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268939 1072 #uuid "dd69890e-422d-56db-a361-c19cc01c3d0a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268939 1074 "To make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $y=20$ on the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/ad/ad092e8773bbb8707dc95377b20b29a1f6ddab9e973b0a5fa469e15f47f967cc.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/52/520c396408909577337035a455f5078df148c491ec9d33a75a0f3bbdcaac9a5f.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/67/67b44784b133813b84204dc63aedf0a07b7edb67a30c52e3dc17d8567fcccb19.png)\n\nTherefore, when $y=20,$ we obtain $x≈7.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268498 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268940 1069 "q-133884" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268940 1071 "![](images/e0/e00248d1dd5cb96a5e0099acf5da41913a805dbc388715f0b542aa821a95efa5.png)\n\nThe scatterplot above shows the relationship between the number of hours $x$ a group of people spend watching TV each night and the time $y$ they sleep each night. The equation of the trend line is $y=10-1.25x.$ \n\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the time a person sleeps per night if they watch TV for $6$ hours." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268940 1072 #uuid "a78cfca9-9001-58c9-bbd8-9235b77891ac" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268940 1074 "To predict the number of hours a person sleeps after watching TV for $6$ hours, we need to substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10-1.25x \\\\  & =10-1.25(6) \\\\  & =10-7.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, a person watching TV for $6$ hours sleeps for approximately $2.5$ hours.\n\nNote: The point where $x=6$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268674 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268941 1069 "q-133928" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268941 1071 "![](images/91/91e15117e7797cfce1b94c33d157c2e89fad519c80c715bfc3ef8082c1e38946.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of a car $y,$ in thousands of dollars, and the number of years $x$ since it was purchased. The equation of the trend line is $y=28-x.$ \n\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the time it will take for the car to be worth $$20\\,000.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268941 1072 #uuid "c98cf0a6-286a-578e-aea8-46d3305548e5" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268941 1074 "To predict the number of years it will take for the car to be worth $$20\\,000,$ we need to substitute $y=20$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =28-x \\\\ 20 & =28-x \\\\ 20+x & =28 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, it will take approximately $8$ years for the car to be worth $$20\\,000.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=20$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268528 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268942 1069 "q-133943" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268942 1071 "![](images/7b/7ba7aa1cf49bb5a2dc430d9a21baaa8491aa61d8898f2aad142406c5c569b51b.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $x$ when $y=7.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,1)$ and $(5,4).$*" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268942 1072 #uuid "b4e2e69a-6742-55aa-baed-902f71aaf13b" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268942 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/59/59efe1b79b0d032179a5210ee694b0d59ad082853ca9fb80a9d79d9f6444f2bf.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,1)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{5-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=0.75$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=0.75x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.75x+b \\\\ 1 & =0.75(1)+b \\\\ 1 & =0.75+b \\\\ b & =0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=0.75x+0.25.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=7$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.75x+0.25 \\\\ 7 & =0.75x+0.25 \\\\ 6.75 & =0.75x \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $y=7$ we have $x≈9.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=7$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268642 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268943 1069 "q-133945" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268943 1071 "![](images/6d/6de719fb9d16173e40759b507f16b0d10cd373059f5d127081d173bc0b3596b1.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $x$ when $y=0.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(21,9)$ and $(25,3).$*" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268943 1072 #uuid "f68e7d84-0059-56aa-a350-f08aac25bccf" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268943 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/35/35797fe2923cdf9c741dda57a40401f4759cd722eb748f80d0df960c5d6eef29.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(21,9)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(25,3).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-9}{25-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{4} \\\\  & =-1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-1.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-1.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(21,9):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1.5x+b \\\\ 9 & =-1.5(21)+b \\\\ 9 & =-31.5+b \\\\ b & =40.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-1.5x+40.5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1.5x+40.5 \\\\ 0 & =-1.5x+40.5 \\\\ 1.5x & =40.5 \\\\ x & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $y=0$ we have $x≈27.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=0$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268751 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268944 1069 "q-133960" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268944 1071 "![](images/00/001019dd20e7a56980201a07e94d3fb9e7824bdeef6864c90abb3172babce734.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the time (in minutes) it takes some professional cyclists to cover particular distances during training. \nUse the trend line to estimate how long it takes one of these cyclists to travel $4$ miles." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268944 1072 #uuid "de4caef5-6cb3-5e27-94ff-951e9f36b643" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268944 1074 "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $y=4$ on the vertical axis.\n\n![](images/19/19cf6fe36f3afb0322e51ab1d9b1d580453380ba67d813a923818756c1d57994.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/88/8815a1433c0c139ecf9370da8a6799b084a0e875a3bddb1b80ffa96792c9a145.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the horizontal axis.\n\n![](images/f1/f183ff82c15c750d47a0da10151917c02311260f0e86daecfeb1b12c9e15007c.png)\n\nTherefore, these cyclists travel $4$ miles in approximately $20$ minutes." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268727 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268945 1069 "q-133961" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268945 1071 "![](images/d3/d3d0f871d60b7f7c8280abd7d3eba4337f910bae6b814586b368bbfb0a8dc313.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the weight loss per week (in pounds) vs. the number of hours spent training per week for a group of people. Use the trend line to estimate how much weight a person loses if they train for $4$ hours per week." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268945 1072 #uuid "81a0f2ef-8e06-5acd-bc49-13e95e8478d8" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268945 1074 "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $x=4$ on the horizontal axis.\n\n![](images/1e/1e397473de815e2eda88ad33c59e2cf77c336b968ef6a1eeaffa67d9fbc64750.png)\n\n- Then, we move up until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/27/27c8ce8a7a1feadd4ea16d66f6a283fa245fa737036810924ead4b0148c6d6f5.png)\n\n- Finally, we move left until we intersect the vertical axis.\n\n![](images/a1/a178070d1f62538a35e7e4e363a254c2afa776920079618b80b3bff358601871.png)\n\nTherefore, a person training $4$ hours a week will lose around $3$ pounds of weight." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268852 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268946 1069 "q-133963" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268946 1071 "A coffee shop owner keeps track of how many cups of coffee are sold each morning during the winter, and the temperature at $7$ a.m. each day. The results for the last $5$ days are shown below.\n\n| **Temperature at \n $7$ a.m. (${}^{∘}C$)** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Cups of Coffee Sold** | $32$ | $28$ | $24$ | $20$ | $16$ |\n\nWhich of the following scatter plots represents this data?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268946 1072 #uuid "79782f5a-3240-5ba8-a5fc-cb108003d3f9" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268946 1074 "We create a scatter diagram in the coordinate plane as follows: \n\n- First, we draw the independent variable (the temperature at $7$ a.m.) along the horizontal axis.\n\n- Then, we draw the dependent variable (number of cups of coffee sold) along the vertical axis.\n\n- Finally, we plot the points that correspond to the pairs from the data.\n\nThis gives us the following scatter plot.\n\n![](images/cc/cc45b5a8b031e01ad71b93c3e809693c2590c691adb98a2872b47c24839c05f7.png)" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268778 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268947 1069 "q-133964" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268947 1071 "![](images/03/032c895f40d741c8468c531d96c763943a391cf7033ca4a63407d6b7b36bacef.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of a motorcycle, in thousands of dollars, several years after it was purchased. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the motorcycle's value $10$ years after it was purchased.\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(2,5)$ and $(4,4).$*" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268947 1072 #uuid "f47a9326-e01b-5e90-a81c-f04764715401" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268947 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/14/145a6a57bcd24c99e7de73f470de61c673b10f5a0d88b0c64527a17e52a0dc86.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-5}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 5 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 5 & =-1+b \\\\ b & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+6.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+6 \\\\  & =-0.5(10)+6 \\\\  & =-5+6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the motorcycle's value $10$ years after it was purchased is around $$1\\,000.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=10$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268607 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268948 1069 "q-133965" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268948 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268948 1071 "![](images/e3/e3319694b1112e92602219492d51116d514a88e885431ac1ef0c8f8becb0dc18.png)\n\nA tea shop wants to determine the relationship between the outside temperature and the number of customers they serve. The scatter plot above shows the information they collected over several days. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the number of customers the tea shop expects to serve if the outside temperature is ${36}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(15,60)$ and $(30,30).$*" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268948 1072 #uuid "9cd1cd6f-81d1-5aa8-a97e-ba1b2bd9d144" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268948 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/7a/7adb5dd9bfcfcea8842eebfcacc704237f010f87c8ffd695afab890801237abd.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(15,60)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(30,30).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-60}{30-15} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-30)}{15} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-2$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(15,60):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+b \\\\ 60 & =-2(15)+b \\\\ 60 & =-30+b \\\\ b & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-2x+90.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=36$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+90 \\\\  & =-2(36)+90 \\\\  & =-72+90 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the tea shop expects to serve $18$ customers.\n\nNote: The point where $x=36$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268949 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268649 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268949 1069 "q-133967" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268949 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268949 1071 "![](images/1d/1d4df2d201ccbaf2305534d61c8a0ed5f7957b1629d8cd2fbd5fff933283c0dc.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the number of matches won by some chess players in a championship and the hours they spend training per day.\nUse the trend line to estimate how much time players need to train each day to win $30$ championship games." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268949 1072 #uuid "d6bab4ea-9515-5432-8897-bfe0c69aa515" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268949 1074 "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $y=30$ on the vertical axis.\n\n![](images/26/26ba10258a8794a97dfc5e61c8160b342ed6890f7a9af40c1e21ac814c779188.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/e5/e50d79adb9803f6509314cf3512fcd388ccc78b287d6653b9e318ce54ce45022.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the horizontal axis.\n\n![](images/a9/a9bd5a7973ecb2e88c328d0943d4bfebe9862b2cea1abbd0b3fa3665fa177167.png)\n\nTherefore, a chess player that wins $30$ games spends around $5$ hours training per day." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268681 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268950 1069 "q-134002" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268950 1071 "![](images/80/80cb125155a1a55404f2257196560a4e563e75c12d4c87c24b0464e509d7a0fa.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot above?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268950 1072 #uuid "a5377839-aece-542c-98bb-f75c10257488" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268950 1074 "The plot shows that the data points are scattered along a straight line.\n\n![](images/18/18cba39701d1ec8ead1281a68fad5dab2b1f584aa396eddcfa306c4961c6397e.png)\n\nWe see that the line $s$ best fits the given data set." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268572 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268951 1069 "q-134006" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268951 1071 "![](images/7e/7e31307366ee31b452ed5c093bfa55c1915b199bf64c5abdf530827e4eb5fdc7.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the grades obtained in science and literature tests among a particular group of students.\nWhich of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot above?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268951 1072 #uuid "e4911a68-5941-50eb-ad52-798a868cf698" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268951 1074 "From the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear trend." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268638 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268952 1069 "q-134008" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268952 1071 "![](images/29/294e45be62260374df597915428bf83ac676ce709d727a7d194755cb73d78b72.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the correspondence between the distance of a house from the nearest public transport facility and its price, in thousands of dollars, among a sample of newly built homes. Which of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268952 1072 #uuid "155124c2-60a1-5157-a592-034e3900447f" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268952 1074 "The plot shows that the data points are scattered along a curve, not a straight line.\n\n![](images/e4/e4654e24215c05554c4985cccc2dcce5f6579de537a6218f4b859f13ff276a29.png)\n\nTherefore, there is no linear trend in the data." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268496 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268953 1069 "q-134009" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268953 1071 "![](images/f6/f64050151e65151975954cdf169379c5ea6ee80b0348a6cb03706ab64c7c7105.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268953 1072 #uuid "595cbfc4-8fff-5045-9e69-dae0c1acb047" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268953 1074 "The plot shows that the data points are scattered along a curve, not a straight line.\n\n![](images/00/00fe583ae8b83bfef31c1914489be7794aa4b7b2850cd01243a46b8d86d1dddb.png)\n\nTherefore, there is no linear trend in the data." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268419 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268954 1069 "q-134011" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268954 1071 "![](images/c5/c55d326b26a8bb6831f38456b7b47878ef66a185090c7327cbb151c4e951be33.png)\n\nWhat is the slope of the trend line shown above?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268954 1072 #uuid "b2869212-3453-5179-ad24-03054774f50a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268954 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,2)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2}{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268447 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268955 1069 "q-134013" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268955 1071 "![](images/72/72a4541fa9349de3a1d7218f3622ebb7536cc460bbf32fd90e43c754fac909a3.png)\n\nWhat is the slope of the trend line shown above?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268955 1072 #uuid "4f18abb5-3d87-50ba-b35e-a057ab7327eb" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268955 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-1)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(3,1).$\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-1)}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268682 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268956 1069 "q-134014" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268956 1071 "Ted plays soccer. The following table shows the ages and corresponding weights of five of Ted's teammates.   \n\n| **Age (years)** | **$10$** | **$11$** | **$12$** | **$13$** | **$14$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight (kg)** | $30$ | $40$ | $40$ | $50$ | $60$ |\n\nWhich of the following scatter plots represents this data?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268956 1072 #uuid "841df1a1-2936-57be-b9b9-393331231f88" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268956 1074 "We create a scatter diagram in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n- First, we draw the independent variable (age) along the horizontal axis.\n\n- Then, we draw the dependent variable (weight) along the vertical axis.\n\n- Finally, we plot the points that correspond to the pairs from the data.\n\nThis gives us the following scatter plot.\n\n![](images/13/135eafa5842b7def7177292f8651834aafb8b4ab446afb676976f3fa50850e5d.png)" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268823 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268957 1069 "q-134016" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268957 1071 "![](images/e2/e2d51071ddc198adecdff56593dec29ba9aef10d90b89ddc03a4cdbe9c757034.png)\n\nWhat is the slope of the trend line shown above?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268957 1072 #uuid "d13468e5-56be-5e98-bed0-f0c775c6fab7" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268957 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,8)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(3,7).$\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-8}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268732 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268958 1069 "q-134018" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268958 1071 "![](images/da/da0836fa401981d7a0a254a136036a0ead7e3be8eefff5ef3fdfb31070759f0a.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the trend line shown above." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268958 1072 #uuid "fd5cfa28-3818-52f3-93a7-964a7ff31464" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268958 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,8).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-3}{5-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=\\frac{5}{4}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{4}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{4}x+b \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{5}{4}(1)+b \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{5}{4}+b \\\\ 12 & =5+4b \\\\ 7 & =4b \\\\ b & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268665 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268959 1069 "q-134019" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268959 1071 "![](images/31/31394106551498e7fd3a5cf57773f11ae31c7bc80c9a12272e95033bdcfd462e.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the trend line shown above." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268959 1072 #uuid "cb94748a-2270-55c2-8da9-a11e4005f88b" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268959 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,9)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,3).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-9}{7-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-\\frac{6}{7}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{6}{7}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(0,9):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{6}{7}x+b \\\\ 9 & =-\\frac{6}{7}(0)+b \\\\ 9 & =0+b \\\\ b & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{6}{7}x+9.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268799 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268960 1069 "q-134023" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268960 1071 "![](images/1c/1c9f30692222eaaed4cda6b608dcc1ccb01eefd6b5fa7730b5b8bb29c9f528b1.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the trend line shown above." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268960 1072 #uuid "1c42b7eb-86b2-59d5-8ce6-728291f9e718" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268960 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,2)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,8).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{7-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=\\frac{6}{5}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6}{5}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{6}{5}x+b \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{6}{5}(2)+b \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{12}{5}+b \\\\ 10 & =12+5b \\\\ -2 & =5b \\\\ b & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268705 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268961 1069 "q-134025" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268961 1071 "![](images/c8/c8f6d0161777412704a9a80c0336af04a6968454d421913055024b6d0cf11c71.png)\n\nEstimate the slope of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round your answer to the nearest $0.5.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268961 1072 #uuid "fec0ce3a-bf15-5c41-9f72-214fd8c31558" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268961 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/b5/b50fd1636cc2e1bb9d150eaba9cc25a3ccedff77e607bf22853004332a7c4ddc.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(3,6)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(5,3).$\n\nTherefore, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{2} \\\\  & =-1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268547 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268962 1069 "q-134026" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268962 1071 "![](images/75/75f8a5bd247b1f792fd60640fbbd2f864359abe48110d59f1fbe0da00eae4ed5.png)\n\nEstimate the slope of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round your answer to the nearest $0.5.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268962 1072 #uuid "0ee24dc0-a479-5765-b9ec-7aa33e9b2391" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268962 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/38/384610cdc149e8b4eaa6df1d3eea1715dda88543c57679553875d226814f6cf7.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(-1,4)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-4}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268462 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268963 1069 "q-134031" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268963 1071 "![](images/11/111833881cf8e939d896f23a5ab7ca436f9265e7b8fd66c099eb118eab8fd6b0.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268963 1072 #uuid "cd67326e-eade-5de0-b602-784a91831a0b" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268963 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/fd/fdad2c939372e14617ba4e393037d7f5852cd8114467aa914ccb8963c0eacfeb.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(2,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(6,1).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-5}{6-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-1$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x+b \\\\ 5 & =-2+b \\\\ 7 & =b \\\\ b & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-x+7.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268581 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268964 1069 "q-134032" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268964 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268964 1071 "![](images/d2/d23d6a35343aebcb986118bd7c5bc720f00480706057ec900e55c75bac296ebb.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268964 1072 #uuid "240005ae-034c-5e2f-9211-125231674030" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268964 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/66/663e4d77d51816641a9fa861f37893d9f986d5e2b00e605ed5eb4fc19d40eab4.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(5,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(7,9).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-5}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=2$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(5,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x+b \\\\ 5 & =2(5)+b \\\\ 5 & =10+b \\\\ -5 & =b \\\\ b & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=2x-5.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268735 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268965 1069 "q-134033" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268965 1071 "![](images/24/24d3507598aa125a515534fd79c82e051875e897bedbe333cdaaf4e4856f67e4.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268965 1072 #uuid "5d2fa245-b431-5e1f-a3e9-29f95f27e80e" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268965 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/f6/f62fe2cf8d93e0737482334a6f659202032160d451e6518afa152f43fb51d0a7.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(2,6)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(4,2).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-6}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-2$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+b \\\\ 6 & =-2(2)+b \\\\ 6 & =-4+b \\\\ 10 & =b \\\\ b & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-2x+10.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268671 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268966 1069 "q-134034" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268966 1071 "The following table indicates the average number of hours per week each student, in a group of five, studies in a semester and the overall percentage each receives for their math classes. \n\n| **Study Time (h)** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Overall Percentage** | $65$ | $60$ | $85$ | $80$ | $90$ |\n\nWhich of the following scatter plots represents this data?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268966 1072 #uuid "1dccd4cc-096b-518a-a4f3-1a9652abb0b6" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268966 1074 "We create a scatter diagram in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n- First, we draw the independent variable (study time) along the horizontal axis.\n\n- Then, we draw the dependent variable (overall percentage) along the vertical axis.\n\n- Finally, we plot the points that correspond to the pairs from the data.\n\nThis gives us the following scatter plot.\n\n![](images/60/6029c42ac9b51e1086ddb519318bc81eb9bc0cda945880f03cd12d3882f3d8f5.png)" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268437 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268967 1069 "q-134043" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268967 1071 "![](images/80/80d08441967f6c1a4026fe9ca0d2bdfdde8fac57a2ae0a7b8d6b34889f3469bd.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between hours of study per week and the overall percentage obtained by students in a math class. What was the overall percentage obtained by the student who studied, on average,  $7$ hours each week?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268967 1072 #uuid "43fcfab7-3240-5e37-8741-3769fb9bd3d5" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268967 1074 "![](images/39/39e7370f9cf517a7a139fecf71561041a9b1470150e0fa333d3fff679f77882d.png)\n\n    \n\nTo determine the overall percentage obtained by the student who studied $7$ hours each week, we locate $7$ on the horizontal axis and move in the vertical direction until we find some points, as shown above.\n\nThe highlighted point has coordinates $(7,85).$ Therefore, the student who studied $7$ hours each week obtained an overall percentage of $85%.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268767 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268968 1069 "q-134055" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268968 1071 "![](images/ed/ed064e23e4d1d11d4e40ef33478eb23860874463d6ce53e6fa3817010a7bd80d.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between heating time and water temperature during an experiment. What is the temperature of the water $4$ minutes after heating has started?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268968 1072 #uuid "6da74fb3-c777-5474-b0fc-337991b39e83" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268968 1074 "![](images/ec/ecedab7bd2fbf79a2fec2e6deabccd292eed5c585efd1268ff675214a0eb2411.png)\n\nTo determine the temperature of the water $4$ minutes after heating has started, we locate $4$ on the horizontal axis and move in the vertical direction until we find some points, as shown above. \n\nThe highlighted point has coordinates $(4,95).$ Therefore, the temperature of the water $4$ minutes after heating has started is $95{\\,}^{∘}C.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268766 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268969 1069 "q-134086" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268969 1071 "![](images/c9/c93f14cd549ddbd849dfed5582bd4f414cf3eac95a4ba2c22d1d4014d3153980.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the number of absences in a month and the size of a class. What is the size of the class that had $7$ absences in the month?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268969 1072 #uuid "d0324225-5474-5b36-bdc0-4adf96d19726" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268969 1074 "![](images/a4/a41ff9a7f77a393b6575ee21525de15517038188afeca0b7783d175c3f4c34b1.png)\n\nTo determine the size of the class that had $7$ absences in a month, we locate $7$ on the vertical axis and move in the horizontal direction until we find some points, as shown above. \n\nThe highlighted point has coordinates $(16,7).$ Therefore, the class that had $7$ absences in the month has $16$ students." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268529 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268970 1069 "q-134087" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268970 1071 "![](images/dc/dc2f3fad9a09ed25fb9e6077069f63aac8afc09090f5453ece33c0b4ab5fb79f.png)\n\nA group of $10$ high school students is surveyed about their study habits. The scatter plot above shows the relationship between the amount of time they watch TV per week and their overall math scores. What is the overall math score of the student who watches TV for $10$ hours per week?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268970 1072 #uuid "ee896e99-9e8c-56c2-b7d5-4f6cd33a16cc" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268970 1074 "![](images/50/500b2e9395d0af1b9ae86ce6e9b874b817690b61faad548c1fa9e33d495a5776.png)\n\nTo determine the overall math score of the student who watches TV for $10$ hours, we locate $10$ on the horizontal axis and move in the vertical direction until we find some points, as shown above.\n\nThe highlighted point has coordinates $(10,75).$ Therefore, the overall math score of the student who watches TV for $10$ hours per week is $75%.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268514 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268971 1069 "q-1341" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268971 1071 "If $\\overset{\\to }{a}=⟨1,-2,4⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{b}=⟨11,4,-1⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{a}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{b}=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268971 1072 #uuid "bd6a5391-fcf3-5a3e-bf4c-ad2c6e4a6452" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268971 1074 "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{a}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 11+(-2)\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =11+(-8)+(-4) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268438 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268972 1069 "q-134105" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268972 1071 "![](images/57/5768d8f90d3dd754471a69d25582eca6cdb2f4993b9b22020dc9f2038aa61954.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above summarizes data collected by a Midwestern high school showing the relationship between the number of absences in a month and the class size. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Only one class reported a total of $6$ absences in the month.\n- Two classes have a total of $20$ students each.\n- Only one class has more than $24$ students." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268972 1072 #uuid "7b463b24-e0d7-56c4-b957-08fba1ac548b" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268972 1074 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is false. The scatter plot has two points whose vertical coordinate is equal to $6,$ namely, $(16,6)$ and $(20,6).$ This means that two of the classes each reported a total of 6 absences in the month.\n\n![](images/a6/a62b1d5a63764b38013fdbdba9ba85a4495cd996a2d9f975dbe3d7eb89166234.png)\n\n  \n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $20,$ namely, $(20,6).$ This means that only one of the classes has a total of  $20$ students.\n\n![](images/3c/3c41f2e111ef141511ec4f2faa655ddcea8f2b7e0b73bd37f0b1e142d5d60f14.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. From the scatter plot, only one class has more than $24$ students. This class is represented by the point to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/ad/adcba37370fdac69b152a8d18ef710d5f665d9a3252376ea312808be7d08a407.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268689 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268973 1069 "q-134128" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268973 1071 "![](images/69/69517e814dae28db96c64a662ce97b5fc40ea10355cb5a02561a0d12e1a3f845.png)\n\nA math teacher asked his students how many hours of sleep they had the\nnight before a test. The information he received is summarized in the scatter plot above.\n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Only one student obtained a score of $90.$\n- Only one student slept for $6$ hours.\n- None of the students obtained a score lower than $75.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268973 1072 #uuid "a188568f-e246-50ee-bc75-14d95b670306" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268973 1074 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $90,$ namely, $(7,90).$ This means that only one student obtained a score of $90.$\n\n![](images/ef/ef915015fa9c7685f45f26ee9cd2c10dfce5a4253aed22b6ea2ea57f0c3c26e2.png)\n\n    \n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $6,$ namely, $(6,80)$ and $(6,85).$ This means that two students had  $6$ hours of sleep.\n\n![](images/31/3139ca71a780b76ca7355ddd119d6502f1cd390d67064db3a4a57801e33f2917.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, three students obtained a score lower than  $75.$  Those students are represented by the three dots below the horizontal line.\n\n![](images/a6/a675a94a9183a86d221bb2199dae38f9f55b9f91dff9868320b0b8f4d38b3fe9.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268477 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268974 1069 "q-134139" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268974 1071 "![](images/37/376bd65db6b1d0a91c6013462e686ffad4d8acceee4948e61cf291d6403a12cd.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above is based on data collected from several homes in a given community. It shows the relationship between the age of a house and the number of people living in it. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There is only one house where precisely $3$ people live.\n- Two of the houses are $16$ years old.\n- Only one house is older than $24$ years old." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268974 1072 #uuid "26f51f13-3f23-54c9-b7ba-52010958ad0a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268974 1074 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $3,$ namely, $(18,3).$ This means that there is only one house where $3$ people live.\n\n![](images/7a/7a56236f0c5649a64436809b2a5e1fb8f00ed3d345cd81c571f96d579d6c19b6.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $16,$ namely, $(16,6).$ This means that only one house is $16$ years old.\n\n![](images/6d/6d9a60cd10c6d7cc9f9d51d0be44c32cb688e19da988b3e84b30f0c4be32a2a8.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, two houses are more than $24$ years old, represented by the two dots to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/44/44b7395bb2e5ee0a71899869e6caedb949e8744adde314134f6a09e4c8f8a35d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268722 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268975 1069 "q-134155" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268975 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={4,\\,6,\\,8,\\,2},\\,y={2,\\,4,\\,10,\\,8}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=6.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268975 1072 #uuid "1d0e8ef1-dbd3-5d5d-b9cb-f3092550c8dd" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268975 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-5)(2-6)+(6-5)(4-6)+(8-5)(10-6)+(2-5)(8-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(-4)+(1)(-2)+(3)(4)+(-3)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[4-2+12-6] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268461 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268976 1069 "q-134156" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268976 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={6,\\,4,\\,6,\\,8},\\,y={3,\\,4,\\,5,\\,8}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=5.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268976 1072 #uuid "0f16abcc-1df4-5043-b12f-f3b3089b65c2" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268976 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(6-6)(3-5)+(4-6)(4-5)+(6-6)(5-5)+(8-6)(8-5)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(0)(-2)+(-2)(-1)+(0)(0)+(2)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[0+2+0+6] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268423 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268977 1069 "q-134157" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268977 1071 "A motorcyclist covers $99$ miles in $3$ hours at a constant rate.  What is the speed of the motorcyclist?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268977 1072 #uuid "59ed4ef6-03e4-56b0-8d48-b111f3f273b4" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268977 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed (in miles per hour), we divide the number of miles, $99,$ by the number of hours, $3.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{99}{3}=99÷3=33.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the motorcyclist is $33$ miles per hour." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268411 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268978 1069 "q-134158" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268978 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={2,\\,4,\\,6,\\,8},\\,y={1,\\,3,\\,5,\\,7}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=4.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268978 1072 #uuid "c6e35293-af9e-5d49-9933-fc37588e5dac" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268978 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2-5)(1-4)+(4-5)(3-4)+(6-5)(5-4)+(8-5)(7-4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(-3)+(-1)(-1)+(1)(1)+(3)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[9+1+1+9] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268602 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268979 1069 "q-134159" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268979 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={-1,\\,6,\\,2,\\,5},\\,y={-2,\\,5,\\,7,\\,6}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=3$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=4.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268979 1072 #uuid "4cf798d3-99fb-51d5-934b-1e9180edf066" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268979 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1-3)(-2-4)+(6-3)(5-4)+(2-3)(7-4)+(5-3)(6-4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-4)(-6)+(3)(1)+(-1)(3)+(2)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[24+3-3+4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268431 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268980 1069 "q-134163" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268980 1071 "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={-2,\\,5,\\,1,\\,4},\\,y={-4,\\,2,\\,4,\\,-6}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268980 1072 #uuid "e7872238-190b-57b6-8044-44a40b6edcd3" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268980 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-2)+5+1+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-4)+2+4+(-6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-2\\,-\\,2)(-4\\,-\\,(-1))+(5\\,-\\,2)(2\\,-\\,(-1))+(1\\,-\\,2)(4\\,-\\,(-1))+(4\\,-\\,2)(-6\\,-\\,(-1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-4)(-3)+(3)(3)+(-1)(5)+(2)(-5)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[12+9-5-10] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268717 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268981 1069 "q-134164" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268981 1071 "A canoe traveled $280$ feet in $70$ seconds at a constant rate.  What is the speed of the canoe?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268981 1072 #uuid "363ac4b8-fa48-5948-b74e-bd2bdaf627fa" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268981 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed (in feet per second), we divide the number of feet, $280,$ by the number of seconds, $70.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{280}{70}=280÷70=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the canoe is $4$ feet per second." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268772 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268982 1069 "q-134165" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268982 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={1,\\,4,\\,7,\\,12},\\,y={2,\\,4,\\,2,\\,8}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=4.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268982 1072 #uuid "a837e578-9d21-592b-8de3-2aa17ce381f9" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268982 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(1-6)(2-4)+(4-6)(4-4)+(7-6)(2-4)+(12-6)(8-4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-5)(-2)+(-2)(0)+(1)(-2)+(6)(4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[10+0-2+24] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268467 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268983 1069 "q-134166" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268983 1071 "A starfish moves $12$ miles in $20$ hours, moving at a constant pace. What is the speed of the starfish?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268983 1072 #uuid "92eb8359-565d-5258-8440-1577884a7d0c" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268983 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed (in miles per hour), we divide the number of miles, $12,$ by the number of hours, $20.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{20} & =\\frac{12÷2}{20÷2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10} \\\\  & =6÷10 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of starfish is $0.6$ miles per hour." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268548 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268984 1069 "q-134168" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268984 1071 "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={-4,\\,2,\\,6,\\,8},\\,y={2,\\,-9,\\,3,\\,12}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268984 1072 #uuid "e3d02030-a1e3-5cc4-b308-6cf0c2ca9175" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268984 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-4)+2+6+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (2+(-9)+3+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-4-3)(2-2)+(2-3)(-9-2)+(6-3)(3-2)+(8-3)(12-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-7)(0)+(-1)(-11)+(3)(1)+(5)(10)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[0+11+3+50] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268667 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268985 1069 "q-134170" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268985 1071 "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={4,\\,-2,\\,5,\\,1},\\,y={-7,\\,5,\\,4,\\,-2}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268985 1072 #uuid "8776d2b8-cc6d-5825-8f19-b2c66c611b9a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268985 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (4+(-2)+5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-7)+5+4+(-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-2)(-7-0)+(-2-2)(5-0)+(5-2)(4-0)+(1-2)(-2-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2)(-7)+(-4)(5)+(3)(4)+(-1)(-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-14-20+12+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-20) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268826 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268986 1069 "q-134172" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268986 1071 "An elevator covers $602$ feet in $4$ minutes, moving at a constant rate. What is the speed of the elevator?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268986 1072 #uuid "9150e0d7-03db-545b-ba25-add48de4f78e" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268986 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed in feet per minute, we divide the number of feet, $602,$ by the number of minutes, $4.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{602}{4} & =\\frac{602÷2}{4÷2} \\\\  & =\\frac{301}{2} \\\\  & =301÷2 \\\\  & =150.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the elevator is $150.5$ feet per minute." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268436 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268987 1069 "q-134173" 13194139533401 1759218