86045585 true] [17592186268988 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, in feet per month, we divide the number of feet $49,$ by the number of months, $14.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{49}{14} & =\\frac{49÷7}{14÷7} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =7÷2 \\\\  & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the glacier is $3.5$ feet per month." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268723 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268989 1069 "q-134176" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268989 1071 "A spacecraft traveled for $15$ seconds at a constant speed of $12$ kilometers per second. Find the distance covered by the spacecraft." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268989 1072 #uuid "51d90e7f-b1f8-520b-8eca-6b795a2f6717" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268989 1074 "The speed is $12$ kilometers per second. To find the distance covered in $15$ seconds, we multiply the speed by $15.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\cdot \\text{time} \\\\  & =12\\times 15 \\\\  & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spacecraft covered $180$ kilometers in $15$ seconds." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268470 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268990 1069 "q-134182" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268990 1071 "It takes $3$ seconds for a peregrine falcon to fly $51$ meters when moving at a constant pace. Find the distance the peregrine falcon covers in $7$ seconds." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268990 1072 #uuid "9ad553fd-b0f2-56fe-ac4f-6eacd4757504" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268990 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed in meters per second, we divide the number of meters $51,$ by the number of seconds, $3.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{51}{3} & =51÷3 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the peregrine is $17$ meters per second. \n\nTo find the distance covered in $7$ minutes, we multiply the speed by $7.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\times \\text{time} \\\\  & =17\\times 7 \\\\  & =119.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the peregrine falcon covers $119$ meters in $7$ seconds." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268641 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268991 1069 "q-134204" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268991 1071 "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight, in kg ($y$)** | $30$ | $34$ | $38$ | $42$ |\n\nTara plays soccer. The table above shows the ages and corresponding weights of four of Tara's teammates.\nCompute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=12$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=36.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268991 1072 #uuid "2b14049c-b60d-5f56-b68b-b2bf296af40c" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268991 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(10\\,-\\,12)(30\\,-\\,36)+(11\\,-\\,12)(34\\,-\\,36)+(12\\,-\\,12)(38\\,-\\,36)+(15\\,-\\,12)(42\\,-\\,36)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-2)(-6)+(-1)(-2)+(0)(2)+(3)(6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[12+2+0+18] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268586 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268992 1069 "q-134207" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268992 1071 "| **Temperature,\nin  ${}^{∘}F\\,(x)$** | $60$ | $42$ | $80$ | $70$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Humidity, in $%\\,(y)$** | $42$ | $24$ | $30$ | $60$ |\n\nThe table above shows data Tom collected about temperature and humidity in his city. \nCompute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=63$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=39.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268992 1072 #uuid "a9a935ad-07e0-5630-a37c-f9c115a6cd5d" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268992 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(60\\,-\\,63)(42\\,-\\,39)+(42\\,-\\,63)(24\\,-\\,39)+(80\\,-\\,63)(30\\,-\\,39)+(70\\,-\\,63)(60\\,-\\,39)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(3)+(-21)(-15)+(17)(-9)+(7)(21)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-9+315-153+147] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 300 \\\\  & =75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268839 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268993 1069 "q-134211" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268993 1071 "| **Time Watching TV per Week, \nin hours ($x$)** | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grades, in percents ($y$)** | $64$ | $78$ | $76$ | $82$ |\n\nThe table above shows the relationship between the amount of time four of Mike's classmates watch TV per week and their overall math scores. Compute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=10$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=75.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268993 1072 #uuid "e60aa99a-1f45-5524-89ca-0cc07165db5c" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268993 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4\\,-\\,10)(64\\,-\\,75)+(8\\,-\\,10)(78\\,-\\,75)+(12\\,-\\,10)(76\\,-\\,75)+(16\\,-\\,10)(82\\,-\\,75)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-6)(-11)+(-2)(3)+(2)(1)+(6)(7)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[66-6+2+42] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 104 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268712 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268994 1069 "q-134212" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268994 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268994 1071 "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $45$ | $39$ | $43$ | $37$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **IQ Score ($y$)** | $90$ | $82$ | $75$ | $85$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages and corresponding IQ scores of some of Jerry's colleagues. Compute the covariance of the data sets." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268994 1072 #uuid "8b56e226-b5b9-5a6b-90ae-c5d764d5437e" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268994 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (45+39+43+37) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 164 \\\\  & =41 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (90+82+75+85) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 332 \\\\  & =83\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(45\\,-\\,41)(90\\,-\\,83)\\,+\\,(39\\,-\\,41)(82\\,-\\,83)\\,+\\,(43\\,-\\,41)(75\\,-\\,83)\\,+\\,(37\\,-\\,41)(85\\,-\\,83)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4)(7)+(-2)(-1)+(2)(-8)+(-4)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[28+2-16-8] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268828 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268995 1069 "q-134216" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268995 1071 "| **Temperature, in ${}^{∘}C$ ($x$)** | $24$ | $26$ | $24$ | $22$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Lemonade Sales, in $$$ ($y$)** | $180$ | $202$ | $190$ | $140$ |\n\nThe owner of a lemonade stand keeps track of how much they sell in relation to the daily temperature. The table above shows the data they collected over the past four days. Compute the covariance of the data sets." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268995 1072 #uuid "1dce4ed9-63fc-595f-be03-a5cf6065880f" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268995 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (24+26+24+22) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 96 \\\\  & =24 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (180+202+190+140) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 712 \\\\  & =178\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(24\\,-\\,24)(180\\,-\\,178)\\,+\\,(26\\,-\\,24)(202\\,-\\,178)\\,+\\,(24\\,-\\,24)(190\\,-\\,178)\\,+\\,(22\\,-\\,24)(140\\,-\\,178)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(0)(2)+(2)(24)+(0)(12)+(-2)(-38)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[0+48+0+76] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 124 \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268559 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268996 1069 "q-134220" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268996 1071 "A jet ski traveled $3\\,960$ meters at a constant speed of $660$ meters per minute. For how long was the jet ski in motion?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268996 1072 #uuid "27bf74aa-5d79-5da2-ae74-80f3b8309260" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268996 1074 "The speed is $660$ meters per minute. To find the time it took the jet ski to cover $3\\,960$ meters, we divide the distance by $660.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,960}{660} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,960÷60}{660÷60} \\\\  & =\\frac{66}{11} \\\\  & =66÷11 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the jet ski was in motion for $6$ minutes." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268430 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268997 1069 "q-134221" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268997 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268997 1071 "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Shoe Size (USA) ($y$)** | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $8$ |\n\nMr. Damon recorded the age and shoe size of four tennis players at his academy. The table above shows the information he collected. Compute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=10$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=8.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268997 1072 #uuid "fd4f8095-844e-57fc-88d9-718d41a408a6" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268997 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(7-10)(7-8)+(9-10)(8-8)+(11-10)(9-8)+(13-10)(8-8)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(-1)+(-1)(0)+(1)(1)+(3)(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[3+0+1+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268699 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268998 1069 "q-134238" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268998 1071 "It takes $4$ hours for a marathon runner to run $20$ miles at a constant pace. How long does it take for the marathon runner to run $26$ miles?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268998 1072 #uuid "0a9b0c15-ba1f-5c7e-978d-afb5cfdec0d9" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268998 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, in miles per hour, we divide the number of miles, which is $20,$ by the number of hours, $4.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{4} & =20÷4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the marathon runner is $5$ miles per hour. \n\nTo find the time it takes the marathon runner to cover $26$ miles, we divide the distance by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{5} \\\\  & =26÷5 \\\\  & =5.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes the marathon runner $5.2$ hours to run $26$ miles." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268811 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268999 1069 "q-134239" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268999 1071 "It takes $6$ hours for a plane to fly $3\\,450$ miles at a constant pace. How long does it take for the plane to fly $115$ miles?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268999 1072 #uuid "43043cf4-c663-552a-93ce-3c216b1d03f4" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186268999 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, in miles per hour, we divide the number of miles, which is $3\\,450,$ by the number of hours, $6.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\,450}{6} & =3\\,450÷6 \\\\  & =575.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the plane is $575$ miles per hour. \n\nTo find the time it takes the plane to cover $115$ miles, we divide the distance by $575.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{115}{575} \\\\  & =\\frac{115÷5}{575÷5} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{115} \\\\  & =\\frac{23÷23}{115÷23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes the plane $0.2$ hours to travel $115$ miles." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268688 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269000 1069 "q-134241" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269000 1071 "It takes $35$ seconds for a dolphin to swim $315$ meters at a constant pace. How long does it take for the dolphin to swim $396$ meters?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269000 1072 #uuid "92c13e99-67cb-5a62-9173-697230856fc4" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269000 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, in meters per second, we divide the number of meters, which is $315,$ by the number of seconds, $35.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{315}{35} & =\\frac{315÷5}{35÷5} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{7} \\\\  & =63÷7 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the dolphin is $9$ meters per second. \n\nTo find the time it takes the dolphin to cover $396$ meters, we divide the distance by $9.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{396}{9} \\\\  & =396÷9 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes the dolphin $44$ seconds to travel $396$ meters." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269001 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268616 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269001 1069 "q-134243" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269001 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269001 1071 "It takes $30$ minutes for a slug to crawl $135$ centimeters when moving at a constant pace. Find the distance this slug covers in $18$ minutes." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269001 1072 #uuid "12110187-5a54-55e6-a927-228b7377faf9" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269001 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed in centimeters per minute, we divide the number of centimeters, $135,$ by the number of minutes, $30.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{135}{30} & =135÷30 \\\\  & =\\frac{135÷15}{30÷15} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =9÷2 \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the slug is $4.5$ centimeters per minute. \n\nTo find the distance covered in $18$ minutes, we multiply the speed by $18.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\times \\text{time} \\\\  & =4.5\\times 18 \\\\  & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slug covers $81$ centimeters in $18$ minutes." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268626 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269002 1069 "q-134529" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269002 1071 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(0.9)^{6}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{6}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269002 1072 #uuid "9041cac6-327c-5f47-8237-615a3a2a4fc4" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269002 1074 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{6} & =1+6x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+6x+15{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{6}≈1+6x+15{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.9)^{6} & =(1+(-0.1))^{6} \\\\  & ≈1+6(-0.1)+15(-0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1-6(0.1)+15(0.01) \\\\  & =1-0.6+0.15 \\\\  & =0.55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(0.9)^{6}-0.55|}{(0.9)^{6}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈3.49%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268558 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269003 1069 "q-134531" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269003 1071 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.02)^{7}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269003 1072 #uuid "5b9f3c5d-56ab-5e0e-af99-3dca08ec2562" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269003 1074 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.02$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.02)^{7} & =(1+0.02)^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(0.02)+21(0.02)^{2} \\\\  & =1+7(0.02)+21(0.000\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.14+0.008\\,4 \\\\  & =1.148\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.02)^{7}-1.148\\,4|}{(1.02)^{7}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.02%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268497 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269004 1069 "q-134533" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269004 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269004 1071 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(2.1)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269004 1072 #uuid "8720e676-8c6a-5823-b428-16ca882e1e2a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269004 1074 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(2)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(2)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(2)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{5}+5{(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(32)+5(16)(x)+10(8)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =32+80x+80{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{5}≈32+80x+80{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.1$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.1)^{5} & =(2+0.1)^{5} \\\\  & ≈32+80(0.1)+80(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =32+80(0.1)+80(0.01) \\\\  & =32+8+0.8 \\\\  & =40.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(2.1)^{5}-40.8|}{(2.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.10%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268781 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269005 1069 "q-134534" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269005 1071 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.98)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to one decimal place." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269005 1072 #uuid "80453bba-d08c-52a7-815e-e04847472e8c" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269005 1074 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(2)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(2)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(2)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{5}+5{(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(32)+5(16)(x)+10(8)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =32+80x+80{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{5}≈32+80x+80{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.02$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.98)^{5} & =(2+(-0.02))^{5} \\\\  & ≈32+80(-0.02)+80(-0.02)^{2} \\\\  & =32-80(0.02)+80(0.000\\,4) \\\\  & =32-1.6+0.032 \\\\  & =30.432\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.98)^{5}-30.432|}{(1.98)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.0%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268603 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269006 1069 "q-134536" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269006 1071 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(2.92)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269006 1072 #uuid "712ada9e-81fc-5e11-8cee-fdab31154765" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269006 1074 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(243)+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.08$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.92)^{5} & =(3+(-0.08))^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(-0.08)+270(-0.08)^{2} \\\\  & =243-405(0.08)+270(0.006\\,4) \\\\  & =243-32.4+1.728 \\\\  & =212.328\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(2.92)^{5}-212.328|}{(2.92)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.02%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268508 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269007 1069 "q-134606" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269007 1071 "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.12)^{7}.$ Give your answer to four decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269007 1072 #uuid "e48f688d-77f5-51b0-b755-536871a592f3" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269007 1074 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.12$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.12)^{7} & =(1+0.12)^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(0.12)+21(0.12)^{2} \\\\  & =1+7(0.12)+21(0.014\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.84+0.302\\,4 \\\\  & =2.142\\,4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto four decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268425 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269008 1069 "q-134609" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269008 1071 "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.94)^{5}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269008 1072 #uuid "55317db7-43a4-5598-8a9b-0907781ceeae" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269008 1074 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}≈1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.06$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.94)^{6} & =(1+(-0.06))^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(-0.06)+10(-0.06)^{2} \\\\  & =1-5(0.06)+10(0.003\\,6) \\\\  & =1-0.3+0.036 \\\\  & =0.736,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268615 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269009 1069 "q-13464" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269009 1071 "Calculate $\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269009 1072 #uuid "9cb5aaff-6467-5367-a002-45fca048bd67" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269009 1074 "Let $u={x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268652 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269010 1069 "q-134647" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269010 1071 "![](images/a1/a1c9ae90281fd3592afbcb2b2330d104c271e694ffca8688e1b4c92af4ce7b71.png)\n\nConsider $△PQR$ shown above. The line segment $\\overset{―}{RH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PQ}.$ If $m∠PQR={109}^{∘},$ calculate $m∠QRH.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269010 1072 #uuid "d7eaa10c-3d9a-5b56-a4df-97162512587b" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269010 1074 "Since the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HQR+m∠PQR & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR+{109}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR & ={71}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $△QHR$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QRH+m∠HQR+m∠RHQ & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{71}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{161}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH & ={19}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268530 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269011 1069 "q-134648" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269011 1071 "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(2.96)^{5}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269011 1072 #uuid "1808e742-3d85-58e3-99dd-e8883935f6ec" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269011 1074 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =243+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.04$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.96)^{5} & =(3+(-0.04))^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(-0.04)+270(-0.04)^{2} \\\\  & =243-405(0.04)+270(0.001\\,6) \\\\  & =243-16.2+0.432 \\\\  & =227.232,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268747 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269012 1069 "q-134649" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269012 1071 "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(4+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(3.89)^{5}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269012 1072 #uuid "49f45853-81b4-5912-ad87-4e38cd015ea8" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269012 1074 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=4,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(4+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(4)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(4)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(4)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(4)}^{5}+5{(4)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(4)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1\\,024+5(256)(x)+10(64)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =1\\,024+1\\,280x+640{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(4+x)}^{5}≈1\\,024+1\\,280x+640{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.11$ into the above approximation of $(4+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.89)^{5} & =(4+(-0.11))^{5} \\\\  & ≈1\\,024+1\\,280(-0.11)+640(-0.11)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,024-1\\,280(0.11)+640(0.012\\,1) \\\\  & =1\\,024-140.8+7.744 \\\\  & =890.944,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269013 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268660 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269013 1069 "q-134653" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269013 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269013 1071 "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2+x)^{8}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.87)^{8}.$ Give your answer to two decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269013 1072 #uuid "b5048af1-9275-52f6-99f4-3cac65ade496" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269013 1074 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=8,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{8} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{0}){(2)}^{8}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{1}){(2)}^{8-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2}){(2)}^{8-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{8}+8{(2)}^{7}{(x)}^{1}+28{(2)}^{6}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =256+8(128)(x)+28(64)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =256+1\\,024x+1\\,792{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{8}≈256+1\\,024x+1\\,792{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.13$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.87)^{8} & =(2+(-0.13))^{8} \\\\  & ≈256+1\\,024(-0.13)+1\\,792(-0.13)^{2} \\\\  & =256-1\\,024(0.13)+1\\,792(0.016\\,9) \\\\  & =256-133.12+30.284\\,8 \\\\  & =153.16,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268708 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269014 1069 "q-1348" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269014 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269014 1071 "Let $p=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ n\\end{aligned}]$ and $q=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Given that $p⊥q$, find the value of $n.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269014 1072 #uuid "214450de-6c4e-5468-90b3-b289da0c12f3" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269014 1074 "We know that $p⊥q$ ($p$ and $q$ are perpendicular) if and only if $p\\cdot q=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 4+n\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =28-4n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 28-4n & =0 \\\\ 28 & =4n \\\\ n & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268632 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269015 1069 "q-13489" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269015 1071 "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $C$ given below. Describe it in terms of ${c}_{ij}.$\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269015 1072 #uuid "4e9da133-83fb-5896-92de-79cce7846abc" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269015 1074 "The entry lies at the intersection of the $1$st row and $1$st column. Hence, we have ${c}_{11}=1.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268582 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269016 1069 "q-13490" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269016 1071 "Find the entry ${g}_{22}$ of the matrix $G$, where \n\n$$\nG=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269016 1072 #uuid "0f770f62-3fda-5a7c-ad0a-d63eb715c9d0" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269016 1074 "The entry ${g}_{22}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $2$nd column. Therefore, ${g}_{22}=2.$\n\n$$\nG=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268779 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269017 1069 "q-135099" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269017 1071 "A marble is projected vertically downward from a building with a velocity of $0.3\\,\\text{km/min}.$  It takes the marble $5\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What is the height of the building? \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269017 1072 #uuid "3e15d21c-4fa7-50e9-9890-e404c2c7a6c8" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269017 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the marble in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The marble falls for $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)(5)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (9.8)(5)^{2} \\\\  & =25+(4.9)(25) \\\\  & =147.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the building is $147.5\\,\\text{m}$ high." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268716 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269018 1069 "q-135104" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269018 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269018 1071 "An apple is projected vertically down from a height of $0.08\\,\\text{km}$ and hits the ground in $4\\,\\text{s}.$ What was the initial velocity of the apple?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269018 1072 #uuid "04fe1ef2-0523-5c15-b02e-4d6d1fa731c2" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269018 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The apple falls for $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- We can find the distance the apple falls in meters as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =0.08\\,\\text{km} \\\\  & =0.08\\,\\text{km}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =0.08\\,\\cancel{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =0.08\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =80\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (80) & =u(4)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\ 80 & =4u+78.4 \\\\ 1.6 & =4u \\\\ u & =0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the apple was $u=0.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269019 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268449 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269019 1069 "q-135223" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269019 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269019 1071 "A coin is projected vertically down from a skyscraper with a height of $720\\,\\text{m}.$ It takes $12\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the coin?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269019 1072 #uuid "59f994d9-de97-52de-9c5c-2b1ac143f4bb" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269019 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The coin falls for $t=12\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The coin covers the distance of $s=720\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (720) & =u(12)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(12)^{2} \\\\ 720 & =12u+705.6 \\\\ 14.4 & =12u \\\\ u & =1.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the coin was $1.2\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268554 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269020 1069 "q-135246" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269020 1071 "![](images/1f/1fa98148cf4302a5524ade655ee09a3f74d248ba766849f62a71f3ec85e860c6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269020 1072 #uuid "f0518adb-cbef-5d73-971c-a5aa9d8f767f" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269020 1074 "In the picture, we are given a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given plane makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268675 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269021 1069 "q-135247" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269021 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269021 1071 "![](images/db/db4700a08fd81ab6d2bc07d80d72fb1fc9edea6c0cdf652b251a755b53a2b148.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269021 1072 #uuid "48cc5ea4-9288-5e4d-b708-8718d21e8b32" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269021 1074 "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $3.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =3.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268817 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269022 1069 "q-135254" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269022 1071 "![](images/61/61b3e9163769acbcb5804ccd2a5cbfed9cbbf2f0fc113ff39b64ea7153fbe2f4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269022 1072 #uuid "2aa5f625-322e-51d6-88b8-f7f203bc5749" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269022 1074 "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $\\sqrt{2}.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268647 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269023 1069 "q-135283" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269023 1071 "![](images/68/68dc8c639235c6dbc2fe9a238a3855327a0c7da337e5792b7896c30e19b2714e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269023 1072 #uuid "b96966b6-94b1-5b8d-8561-af71f82f4780" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269023 1074 "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the ot