592186269028 1072 #uuid "f6e0d64d-6adc-551a-b870-a3578b510cca" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269028 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](images/00/0043ffdc885e7edeebb7c4df571466fe4f13dfb08728290cd6d54f47777a7179.png)" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269029 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268713 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269029 1069 "q-135303" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269029 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269029 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =0$ in spherical polar coordinates?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269029 1072 #uuid "89a2bbd2-2dd6-5b47-be2c-faba4acc741a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269029 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =0\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $0.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](images/83/832655d3a5ee1429fb1e1c005faa8c19d2a92ecfcf00cef64cfb165bf4755729.png)" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269030 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268633 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269030 1069 "q-135306" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269030 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269030 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.85\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269030 1072 #uuid "c393953b-f9cd-55bd-bdae-3a3a7283cff2" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269030 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.85\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.4}{7}+\\frac{0.6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{0.6}{7}+\\frac{3.4}{7}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269031 1069 "q-135307" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269031 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269031 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269031 1072 #uuid "56012d7d-0dc5-5803-80a1-167efecb090a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269031 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.02+0.24 \\\\ 0.18+0.56\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.26 \\\\ 0.74\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269032 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268648 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269032 1069 "q-135310" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269032 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269032 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 