mine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.03+0.105+0.12 \\\\ 0.06+0.49+0.07 \\\\ 0.01+0.105+0.01\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.255 \\\\ 0.62 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268428 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269034 1069 "q-135316" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269034 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269034 1071 "Find the inverse of the following matrix: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269034 1072 #uuid "1439b942-0950-5593-8d8d-9733c2503b7e" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269034 1074 "Notice that the given matrix is diagonal.\n\nThe inverse of a diagonal matrix can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{a}_{11}} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{{a}_{22}} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & \\frac{1}{{a}_{nn}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269035 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268690 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269035 1069 "q-135317" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269035 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269035 1071 "Find the inverse of the following matrix: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269035 1072 #uuid "8095d1b2-ae71-579f-92b3-52d5ee417ec5" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269035 1074 "Notice that the given matrix is diagonal.\n\nThe inverse of a diagonal matrix can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{a}_{11}} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{{a}_{22}} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & \\frac{1}{{a}_{nn}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269036 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268493 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269036 1069 "q-135323" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269036 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269036 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.4\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269036 1072 #uuid "d5136e32-dc7f-55de-b02b-4d944cd2c122" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269036 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.75{x}_{1}+0.6{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 75{x}_{1}-60{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{5}{9}=-\\frac{1}{9}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269037 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268840 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269037 1069 "q-135324" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269037 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269037 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.1\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the stea