=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{13} \\\\ \\frac{10}{13}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{3}{13}-\\frac{10}{13}=-\\frac{7}{13}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269039 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268531 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269039 1069 "q-13533" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269039 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269039 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269039 1072 #uuid "5d3c5f6c-be88-5a7d-88c2-f5f9124aa97d" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269039 1074 "Let $u=2{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{\\pi /2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268635 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269040 1069 "q-135330" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269040 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 1 \\\\ 0.65 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-