n the main diagonal are ones\n\n- the matrix has only one column (the $1$st column) where some entries below the main diagonal are not zeros\n\n- all the other entries outside the main diagonal are zeros\n\nTo find the inverse of a Frobenius matrix, we simply take the non-zero entries outside the main diagonal with opposite signs. \n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268837 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269060 1069 "q-135397" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269060 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269060 1071 "Find the inverse of the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269060 1072 #uuid "1558931d-a4e9-504d-acf2-215d0c209035" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269060 1074 "Notice that we're given a Frobenius matrix:\n\n- all entries on the main diagonal are ones\n\n- the matrix has only one column (the $1$st column) where some entries below the main diagonal are not zeros\n\n- all the other entries outside the main diagonal are zeros\n\nTo find the inverse of a Frobenius matrix, we simply take the non-zero entries outside the main diagonal with opposite signs. \n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268458 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269061 1069 "q-135407" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269061 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269061 1071 "A town planning department wants to know how its citizens' preferences for traveling to work are changing. So they conducted a survey, and the results are shown below.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Car** | **Subway** |\n| To: | **Car** | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| **Subway** | $0.5$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nThey found that $50%$ of those surveyed that currently drive to work are planning to switch to the subway next year, while $60%$ of the people surveyed that currently use the subway are planning to switch to their cars next year.\n\nAssuming that the town has $50\\,000$ that travel to work by car or subway, and the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many people will eventually use their cars to drive to work? Round your answer to the nearest hundreds." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269061 1072 #uuid "35bb1a67-ff7a-5da5-9b52-f94711e5bbed" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269061 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.5 & -0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{11}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{11} \\\\ \\frac{5}{11}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{6}{11}$ of the $50\\,000$ people surveyed would use their cars. Therefore, the total number of people who will eventually use their cars is\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{11}\\cdot 50\\,000≈27\\,300.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269062 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268656 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269062 1069 "q-135408" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269062 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269062 1071 "There are two groups of people In a small town: those who travel to work by car and those who travel by train. Every year a study is done in which people are asked about their preference between using trains versus cars. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Car** | **Train** |\n| To: | **Car** | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Train** | $0.65$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $65%$ of people who currently travel by car will switch to train next year, while $25%$ of people who currently travel by train will switch to their car next year.\n\nAssuming that the town has $90\\,000$ people that travel to work by train or car, and the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many people will eventually use the train to travel to work?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269062 1072 #uuid "a2ead374-20fc-5336-8f26-ef768edbfcce" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269062 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.75\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.75\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.65 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & -0.25\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.65 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 0.65 & -0.25 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.65 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{18}{13}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{13}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ \\frac{13}{18}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{13}{18}$ of the people will eventually use the train to travel. Therefore, the number of people who will eventually use the train is\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{18}\\cdot 90\\,000=65\\,000.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268613 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269063 1069 "q-135409" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269063 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269063 1071 "A factory produces two types of sugar: brown and extra-refined. Every year, the factory's sales executives study how customers' preferences change between the two types of sugar. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Brown** | **Extra-Refined** |\n| To: | **Brown** | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n| **Extra-Refined** | $0.2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $20%$ of customers who currently buy brown sugar will switch to extra-refined sugar next year, while $100%$ of customers who currently purchase extra-refined sugar will switch to brown sugar next year.\n\nAssuming that there are $450\\,000$ customers in total and that the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many customers will eventually consume extra-refined sugar?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269063 1072 #uuid "b9da89ba-6ad8-53a7-ac87-9c6b7aab9eb4" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269063 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n{P}^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0.84 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.16 & 0.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=5{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the customers will eventually use extra-refined sugar. Therefore, the number of customers who will eventually use this type of sugar is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 450\\,000=75\\,000.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268761 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269064 1069 "q-13543" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269064 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269064 1071 "Find $M+O-(N+M)$, if\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,N=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,O=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269064 1072 #uuid "52150036-21b2-593f-96c3-821ddd81a9cf" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269064 1074 "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M+O-(N+M) & =M+O-N-M \\\\  & =\\cancel{M}+O-N-\\cancel{M} \\\\  & =O-N\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O-N & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8-1 & 6-4 \\\\ 5-2 & 3-(-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268612 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269065 1069 "q-135432" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269065 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269065 1071 "A company processes and packages three types of rice: rice $A,$ rice $B,$ and rice $C.$ A study by the company's sales analysts demonstrates how consumer preference changes from one kind of rice to another year after year. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rice $A$** | **Rice $B$** | **Rice $C$** |\n| To: | **Rice $A$** | $0.5$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| **Rice $B$** | $0.2$ | $0.7$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Rice $C$** | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.65$ |  |\n\nFor example, $20%$ of the people who preferred rice $B$ last year switched to rice $C$ this year.\n\nIf the company has $200\\,000$ customers and the listed probabilities remain constant over many years, how many customers will eventually purchase rice $B?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269065 1072 #uuid "4d1e3f01-76a8-5dc2-a5d5-825db1b3cf60" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269065 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.7 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.7 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.1 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & -0.35\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.1 & 0.15 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.3 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & -0.35 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=100{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=100{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 1.5 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -30 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 30 & 20 & -35 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 1.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -26 & 26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 26 & -26 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 1.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -26 & 26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-5{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{2}{5}$ of the customers will consume rice $B.$ Therefore, the number of people who will eventually consume rice $B$ will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot 200\\,000=80\\,000.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268775 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269066 1069 "q-135433" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269066 1071 "A study has recorded and analyzed the results of the parliamentary elections of a particular country, held every four years. The study shows how voter preference changes from one political party to another. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Yellow Party** | **Blue Party** | **Red Party** |\n| To: | **Yellow Party** | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Blue Party** | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $0.3$ |  |\n| **Red Party** | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $10%$ of the people who voted for the yellow party in the last election will vote for the red party in the next election.\n\nIf the electorate contains $5.6$ million voters and the given probabilities remain constant election after election, how many people will vote for the red party after many elections have taken place?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269066 1072 #uuid "fabc74b8-a791-5627-801a-2888d8f737ff" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269066 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.8 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many elections, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.8 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.3 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.3 & 0.1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.2 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -15 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3}-6{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{4}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}_{3}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 7{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{28} \\\\ \\frac{15}{28} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many elections, $\\frac{1}{7}$ of the people will vote for the red party. Therefore, the number of people that will vote for the red party equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 5.6\\,\\text{million}=0.8\\,\\text{million}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268654 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269067 1069 "q-135441" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269067 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269067 1072 #uuid "9f4184b0-1aa9-527d-b4bd-2a5a8359efb0" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269067 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{2,3}=5\\pm 7\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{2,3}=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268509 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269068 1069 "q-135442" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269068 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{1}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269068 1072 #uuid "b1f7edab-6366-5d37-a398-f06e27973949" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269068 1074 "To find the state vector ${x}_{1},$ we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.43 \\\\ 0.28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268464 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269069 1069 "q-135447" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269069 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.8\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{1}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269069 1072 #uuid "91c34446-e408-527c-8e39-173a34804bb1" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269069 1074 "To find the state vector ${x}_{1},$ we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.17 \\\\ 0.83\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268608 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269070 1069 "q-135450" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269070 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.6\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269070 1072 #uuid "643d92cb-d46f-530f-8315-820914561488" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269070 1074 "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.38 \\\\ 0.62\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.38 \\\\ 0.62\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.324 \\\\ 0.676\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268856 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269071 1069 "q-135458" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269071 1071 "A certain company produces two types of motorcycles: classic and high-cylinder. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of motorcycles each decade:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Classic** | **High-Cylinder** |\n| To: | **Classic** | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |\n| **High-Cylinder** | $0.4$ | $0.8$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of classic motorcycle customers switch to high-cylinder each decade, and $20%$ of high-cylinder motorcycle customers switch to Classic.\n\nCurrently, $7\\,000$ customers use classic motorcycles while $3\\,000$ customers use high-cylinder motorcycles. How many customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles in one decade? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269071 1072 #uuid "bbeb73de-fe7f-52b6-aff7-483b9a90311b" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269071 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of motorcycle is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7\\,000 \\\\ 3\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(10\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{10\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}7\\,000 \\\\ 3\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one decade, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.48 \\\\ 0.52\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one decade, $52%$ of customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles. Computing $52%$ of $10\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot 0.52=5\\,200.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5\\,200$ customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles in one decade." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268473 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269072 1069 "q-135460" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269072 1071 "A certain company produces three types of milk: whole, skim, and lactose-free. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of milk each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Whole** | **Skim** | **Lactose-free** |\n| To: | **Whole** | $0.5$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Skim** | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Lactose-free** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of whole milk customers switch to skim milk each year, and $20%$ of lactose-free milk customers switch to whole milk.\n\nCurrently, $400\\,000$ customers consume whole milk, $400\\,000$ customers consume skim milk, and $200\\,000$ customers consume lactose-free milk. How many customers will consume whole milk after one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269072 1072 #uuid "9699dcc6-557c-5bb4-a953-a7c82014e4b2" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269072 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of milk is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}400\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}400\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.36 \\\\ 0.36 \\\\ 0.28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $36%$ of customers will consume whole milk. Computing $36%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.36=360\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360\\,000$ customers will consume whole milk after one year." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268413 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269073 1069 "q-135465" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269073 1071 "A certain company produces three types of ground coffee: fine, medium, and coarse. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of ground coffee each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Fine** | **Medium** | **Coarse** |\n| To: | **Fine** | $0.5$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Medium** | $0.3$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Coarse** | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of fine ground coffee customers switch to medium ground coffee each year, and $20%$ of coarse ground coffee customers switch to fine ground coffee.\n\nCurrently, $300\\,000$ customers buy fine ground coffee, $500\\,000$ customers buy medium ground coffee, and $200\\,000$ customers buy coarse ground coffee. How many customers will purchase fine ground coffee in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269073 1072 #uuid "25a6f23d-99a0-510e-abf4-afb5461a1de7" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269073 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of ground coffee is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}300\\,000 \\\\ 500\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}300\\,000 \\\\ 500\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.47 \\\\ 0.24\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $29%$ of customers will buy fine ground coffee. Computing $29%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.29=290\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $290\\,000$ customers will buy fine ground coffee in one year." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268703 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269074 1069 "q-135466" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269074 1071 "A certain cinema sells three types of popcorn: traditional, butter, and caramelized. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of popcorn each month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Traditional** | **Butter** | **Caramelized** |\n| To: | **Traditional** | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Butter** | $0.3$ | $0.5$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Caramelized** | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of traditional popcorn customers switch to butter popcorn each month, and $40%$ of butter popcorn customers switch to caramelized popcorn.\n\nCurrently, $400$ customers buy traditional popcorn, $300$ customers buy butter popcorn, and $300$ customers buy caramelized popcorn. How many customers will purchase butter popcorn in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269074 1072 #uuid "9bc1baec-638c-5833-91cf-cfdafab596c1" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269074 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of popcorn customers is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 300 \\\\ 300\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 300 \\\\ 300\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ 0.33 \\\\ 0.42\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $33%$ of customers will buy butter popcorn. Computing $33%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\cdot 0.33=330.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $330$ customers will buy butter popcorn in one month." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268756 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269075 1069 "q-135467" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269075 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269075 1072 #uuid "bef37eb1-45d3-5a03-bda7-0732ce91a120" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269075 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=8\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{1,2}=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ ∓7\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268768 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269076 1069 "q-135483" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269076 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269076 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}0.45 \\\\ 0.55 \\\\ -0.05\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0.65 \\\\ 0.15 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269076 1072 #uuid "2df67835-850a-53e8-b383-b1ca105c4466" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269076 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $v$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.7+0.3=1\n$$\n\n- Vector $w$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.65+0.15+0.2=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$v$ and $w$ only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268527 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269077 1069 "q-135484" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269077 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}0.15 \\\\ 0.85\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 \\\\ -0.1 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269077 1072 #uuid "0039e727-f97e-5ab6-aa21-97ec0dadd8d9" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269077 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.15+0.85=1\n$$\n\n- Vector $v$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $w$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.2+0.3+0.5=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$u$ and $w$ only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268517 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269078 1069 "q-135485" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269078 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269078 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ -0.1 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0.15 \\\\ 0.95\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269078 1072 #uuid "11a06254-1131-54e3-ac79-433bb7764638" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269078 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $v$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $w$is *not* a stochastic vector since the entries do not add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.15+0.95=1.1\\ne 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268683 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269079 1069 "q-135488" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269079 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0 & 0.9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.9 & 0 & 0.1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269079 1072 #uuid "7cb13afc-7824-57e2-abcc-e880dfaafeec" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269079 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $U$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its second column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0 & 0.9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.9 & 0 & 0.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $V$  is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $W$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are non-negative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.5+0.5=1 \\\\ 0.3+0.7=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268696 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269080 1069 "q-135529" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269080 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269080 1072 #uuid "5999fc8e-7257-5b70-9229-12117f86cb5c" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269080 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-1(-5)) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=2.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-6\\pm \\sqrt{(6)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 13}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268743 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269081 1069 "q-135530" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269081 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269081 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269081 1072 #uuid "62cbd463-845c-5e66-8284-1a8dd6e26538" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269081 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )^{2}-4(-9)) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=5.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 40}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-144}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 12\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pm 6\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268598 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269082 1069 "q-135532" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269082 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269082 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 9 \