9087 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269087 1071 "$[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269087 1072 #uuid "46a7086f-e5e2-507c-96e3-ccbdd5bdc78c" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269087 1074 "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot 3+2\\cdot -2 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+5\\cdot -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268631 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269088 1069 "q-135554" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269088 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269088 1071 "There are two groups of children in a certain city: those who prefer ice cream and those who prefer cakes. A study has shown that, each week\n\n- from the children that prefer ice cream, $35%$ change to cakes, and\n\n- from the children that prefer cakes, $42%$ change to ice cream.\n\nWhich of the following tables describes how these children change their preferences each week?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269088 1072 #uuid "9a74ecb8-81c8-5154-b418-5e469935a079" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269088 1074 "We are given the following situation for each week: \n\n- $35%$ of children who preferred ice creams swap to cakes. This implies that $65%$ of them continue to prefer ice creams.\n\n- $42%$ of children who preferred cakes swap to ice creams. This implies that $58%$ of them continue to prefer cakes.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Ice-creams** | **Cakes** |\n| To: | **Ice-creams** | $0.65$ | $0.42$ |\n| **Cakes** | $0.35$ | $0.58$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.65 & 0.42 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.58\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268623 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269089 1069 "q-135565" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269089 1071 "A study shows that for a particular area in a specific season,\n\n- on a day that follows a day without precipitation, there is a $25%$ chance to have rain and a $35%$ chance to have snow.\n\n- on a day that follows a rainy day, there is a $40%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have snow.\n\n- on a day that follows a snowy day, there is a $50%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have rain.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.25 & b & 0.05 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.05 & c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269089 1072 #uuid "16f6ef41-f784-5aac-bb4c-b2858ff4b290" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269089 1074 "We are given the following situation for this area:\n\n- On a day that follows a day without precipitation, there is a $25%$ chance to have rain and a $35%$ chance to have snow. This implies a $40%$ chance of not having precipitation on this day.\n\n- On a day that follows a rainy day, there is a $40%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have snow. This implies a $55%$ chance of having rain on this day.\n\n- On a day that follows a snowy day, there is a $50%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have rain. This implies a $45%$ chance of having snow on this day.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **No Precipitation** | **Rain** | **Snow** |\n| To: | **No Precipitation** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| **Rain** | $0.25$ | $0.55$ | $0.05$ |  |\n| **Snow** | $0.35$ | $0.05$ | $0.45$ |  |\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.25 & 0.55 & 0.05 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.05 & 0.45\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.4+0.55+0.45=1.4.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268825 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269090 1069 "q-135566" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269090 1071 "There are three cable TV providers in a particular apartment block: provider $A,$ provider $B,$ and provider $C.$ A study has shown that, each year\n\n- from the tenants using provider $A,$ $80%$ swap to provider $B$ and $10%$ swap to provider $C,$\n\n- from the tenants using provider $B,$ $25%$ swap to provider $A$ and $25%$ swap to provider $C,$\n\n- from the tenants using provider $C,$ $70%$ swap to provider $A$ and $10%$ swap to provider $B.$\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & a & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5 & b \\\\ c & 0.25 & 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269090 1072 #uuid "40ee85ea-08ea-5955-8652-e4607e79ae86" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269090 1074 "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- From the tenants using provider $A,$ $80%$ swap to provider $B$ and $10%$ swap to provider $C.$  This implies that $10%$ of them continue using provider $A.$\n\n- From the tenants using provider $B,$ $25%$ swap to provider $A$ and $25%$ swap to provider $C.$ This implies that $50%$ of them continue using provider $B.$\n\n- From the tenants using provider $C,$ $70%$ swap to provider $A$ and $10%$ swap to provider $B.$ This implies that $20%$ of them continue using provider $C.$\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Provider A** | **Provider B** | **Provider C** |\n| To: | **Provider A** | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Provider B** | $0.8$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ |  |\n| **Provider C** | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.25 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.25 & 0.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.25+0.1+0.1=0.45.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268490 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269091 1069 "q-13565" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269091 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269091 1071 "Find $Hn$ if \n$H=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$n=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269091 1072 #uuid "9a02491f-bea4-5783-964c-e0eaa7500dd1" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269091 1074 "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(-7)\\cdot -3+2\\cdot 2 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 2\\cdot -3+(-5)\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$Hn=[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268414 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269092 1069 "q-135660" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269092 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269092 1071 "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\text{i} \\\\ 3-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+6\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$  respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269092 1072 #uuid "2473b775-36eb-5e2f-9d42-ae0339f64aa2" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269092 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268847 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269093 1069 "q-135667" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269093 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269093 1071 "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2+5\\text{i} \\\\ 1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4-7\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2+7\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269093 1072 #uuid "47b1e90f-9592-5fb0-9bb9-cad4410fafa1" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269093 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268479 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269094 1069 "q-135988" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269094 1071 "${(1+5z)}^{8}=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269094 1072 #uuid "5c7ec2a1-9cec-50e2-a260-5947222136a4" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269094 1074 "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=5z$ and $n=8$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+5z)}^{8} & =1+8\\cdot 5z+\\frac{8(8-1)}{2!}\\cdot {(5z)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+40z+\\frac{8\\cdot 7}{2}\\cdot 25{z}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+40z+28\\cdot 25{z}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+40z+700{z}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268500 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269095 1069 "q-135990" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269095 1071 "${(1-5t)}^{7}=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269095 1072 #uuid "5d179469-7f8f-5cce-bde9-9a8db5183034" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269095 1074 "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=-5t$ and $n=7$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1-5t)}^{7} & =1+7\\cdot (-5t)+\\frac{7(7-1)}{2!}\\cdot (-5t)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-35t+\\frac{7\\cdot 6}{2}\\cdot 25{t}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-35t+21\\cdot 25{t}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-35t+525{t}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268491 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269096 1069 "q-135993" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269096 1071 "${(1-\\frac{b}{3})}^{8}=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269096 1072 #uuid "c9acabc5-eefe-5fc5-86d6-51f1a675d4e4" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269096 1074 "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=-\\frac{b}{3}$ and $n=8$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1-\\frac{b}{3})}^{8} & =1+8\\cdot (-\\frac{b}{3})+\\frac{8(8-1)}{2!}\\cdot {(-\\frac{b}{3})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{8b}{3}+\\frac{8\\cdot 7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{b}^{2}}{9}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{8b}{3}+28\\cdot \\frac{{b}^{2}}{9}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{8b}{3}+\\frac{28{b}^{2}}{9}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268685 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269097 1069 "q-135995" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269097 1071 "${(1-\\frac{2k}{3})}^{7}=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269097 1072 #uuid "1e2921e3-3663-532f-adb3-94884fb8e499" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269097 1074 "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=-\\frac{2k}{3}$ and $n=7$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1-\\frac{2k}{3})}^{7} & =1+7\\cdot (-\\frac{2k}{3})+\\frac{7(7-1)}{2!}\\cdot {(-\\frac{2k}{3})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{14k}{3}+\\frac{7\\cdot 6}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4{k}^{2}}{9}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{14k}{3}+21\\cdot \\frac{4{k}^{2}}{9}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{14k}{3}+\\frac{28{k}^{2}}{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268754 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269098 1069 "q-135997" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269098 1071 "${(1-\\frac{5c}{2})}^{6}=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269098 1072 #uuid "826c8bb8-d166-5c58-b07f-9be615cefc9a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269098 1074 "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=-\\frac{5c}{2}$ and $n=6$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1-\\frac{5c}{2})}^{6} & =1+6\\cdot (-\\frac{5c}{2})+\\frac{6(6-1)}{2!}\\cdot {(-\\frac{5c}{2})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-15c+\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{25{c}^{2}}{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-15c+15\\cdot \\frac{25{c}^{2}}{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-15c+\\frac{375{c}^{2}}{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268680 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269099 1069 "q-136000" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269099 1071 "$(5+x)^{3}=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269099 1072 #uuid "d8689479-aefe-5863-a390-da815632fc3f" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269099 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of $(5+x)^{3},$ we have  $a=5,$ $b=x,$ $n=3.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of $(5+x)^{3}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(5+x)}^{3} & =(\\frac{3}{0}){(5)}^{3}{(x)}^{0}+(\\frac{3}{1}){(5)}^{3-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\frac{3}{2}){(5)}^{3-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(5)}^{3}+3{(5)}^{2}{(x)}^{1}+3{(5)}^{1}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =125+75x+15{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268829 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269100 1069 "q-136002" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269100 1071 "$(3+x)^{4}=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269100 1072 #uuid "7cf853ca-6a16-5936-9604-4bf59d63715b" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269100 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of $(3+x)^{4},$ we have  $a=3,$ $b=x,$ $n=4.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of $(3+x)^{4}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{4} & =(\\frac{4}{0}){(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\frac{4}{1}){(3)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\frac{4}{2}){(3)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{4}+4{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(3)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =81+108x+54{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268503 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269101 1069 "q-136006" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [1759