5}+5(2x)^{4}(-y)+10(2x)^{3}(-y)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =32{x}^{5}-80{x}^{4}y+80{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268776 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269105 1069 "q-136014" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269105 1071 "The coefficient of ${a}^{3}$ in the expansion of ${(1-\\frac{a}{2})}^{14}$ is" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269105 1072 #uuid "dd5426c2-f89c-51fb-b15d-1c1556603be5" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269105 1074 "The binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nIn particular, the ${x}^{3}$ term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{3!}{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-\\frac{a}{2}$ and $n$ with $14$ in the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14(14-1)(14-2)}{3!}\\cdot {(-\\frac{a}{2})}^{3} & =\\frac{14\\cdot 13\\cdot 12}{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}})\\cdot {a}^{3} \\\\  & =364\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}){a}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{91}{2}{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${a}^{3}$ is $-\\frac{91}{2}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268568 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269106 1069 "q-136015" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269106 1071 "${(x-y)}^{8}=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269106 1072 #uuid "4bfb4f9e-e9db-5bd6-a1f7-0c71987ddffc" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269106 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x-y)}^{8},$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=-y,$ $n=8.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x-y)}^{8}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-y)}^{8} & =(\\frac{8}{0}){(x)}^{8}{(-y)}^{0}+(\\frac{8}{1}){(x)}^{8-1}{(-y)}^{1}+(\\frac{8}{2}){(x)}^{8-2}{(-y)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(x)}^{8}+8{(x)}^{7}{(-y)}^{1}+28{(x)}^{6}{(-y)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & ={x}^{8}-8{x}^{7}y+28{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268521 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269107 1069 "q-136018" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269107 1071 "The coefficient of ${b}^{4}$ in the expansion of ${(1-\\frac{b}{3})}^{9}$ is" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269107 1072 #uuid "f4e7d779-c1af-5fa9-8575-868fa38eb63e" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269107 1074 "The binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nIn particular, the ${x}^{4}$ term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)(n-3)}{4!}{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-\\frac{b}{3}$ and $n$ with $9$ in the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9(9-1)(9-2)(9-3)}{4!}\\cdot {(-\\frac{b}{3})}^{4} & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6}{24}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}\\cdot {b}^{4} \\\\  & =126\\cdot \\frac{1}{81}{b}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{9}{b}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${b}^{4}$ is $\\frac{14}{9}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268463 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269108 1069 "q-136020" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269108 1071 "The coefficient of ${z}^{4}$ in the expansion of ${(1-2z)}^{7}$ is" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269108 1072 #uuid "038f6cf1-25e7-5c34-bc07-a180a02393d4" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269108 1074 "The binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nIn particular, the ${x}^{4}$ term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)(n-3)}{4!}{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-2z$ and $n$ with $7$ in the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7(7-1)(7-2)(7-3)}{4!}\\cdot (-2z)^{4} & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4}{24}\\cdot (-2)^{4}\\cdot {z}^{4} \\\\  & =35\\cdot 16{z}^{4} \\\\  & =560{z}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${z}^{4}$ is $560.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268532 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269109 1069 "q-13613" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269109 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-23x+10?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269109 1072 #uuid "54404730-020a-547b-8cd2-8751476ed4d0" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269109 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $3$ does not take the above form. The value $3$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{3}{1},$ and the numerator $(3)$ is not a factor of $10.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268453 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269110 1069 "q-13616" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269110 1071 "![](images/af/afef278ec13dda23227ef9dc28b0e5f3240a0481eaca208fa3b2d3ba27c85537.png)\n\nGiven that $(-1,4)$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ above, find the coordinates of $P.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269110 1072 #uuid "b044ef6d-ccc6-51e6-8baa-a24d9d84225e" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269110 1074 "The given points are ${{(2,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$, ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(-1,4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (-1,4) & =(\\frac{2+a}{2},\\frac{2+b}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-1=\\frac{2+a}{2} \\\\ 4=\\frac{2+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}a=-4 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-4,6).$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268794 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269111 1069 "q-13629" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269111 1071 "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269111 1072 #uuid "58bc7b72-2a9f-540e-8bdb-e3a88a2c71cf" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269111 1074 "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 5\\cdot 5+(-3)\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268522 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269112 1069 "q-136315" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269112 1071 "A particle moves with a constant velocity of $u=-2i\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Find the speed of the particle." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269112 1072 #uuid "0cdc08cd-e7aa-56f0-a558-4fe27881e8db" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269112 1074 "The speed of a particle equals the magnitude of its velocity.\n\nTherefore, the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u| & =|-2i| \\\\  & =|-2| \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nkilometers per hour." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268721 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269113 1069 "q-136320" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269113 1071 "A particle moves with a constant velocity of $u=-7j\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Find the speed of the particle." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269113 1072 #uuid "ca6021dc-32af-5344-a2a8-e054af092e66" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269113 1074 "The speed of a particle equals the magnitude of its velocity.\n\nTherefore, the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u| & =|-7j| \\\\  & =|-7| \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nkilometers per hour." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268450 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269114 1069 "q-136337" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269114 1071 "The velocity of a particle is $(5i-3j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ If the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(i+2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ find the velocity of the particle when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269114 1072 #uuid "ce0fd274-5b83-5bcc-836e-cba4e455a3af" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269114 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu=5i-3j,\\,a=i+2j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(5i-3j)+(i+2j)(2) \\\\  & =(5i-3j)+(2i+4j) \\\\  & =(7i+j)\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268659 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269115 1069 "q-136342" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269115 1071 "The velocity of a particle is $(5i+j)\\,\\text{km/h}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{h}.$ If the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $3j\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ find the velocity of the particle when $t=4\\,\\text{h}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269115 1072 #uuid "7a637fe5-10be-58fa-8ea7-d5ae4409751a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269115 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu=5i+j,\\,a=3j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t=4\\,\\text{h},$ the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(5i+j)+(3j)(4) \\\\  & =(5i+j)+(12j) \\\\  & =(5i+13j)\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268738 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269116 1069 "q-136365" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269116 1071 "A particle moves with constant acceleration. When $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle is $(i-6j)\\,\\text{m/s},$ and when $t=3\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle is $(4i+3j)\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Calculate the acceleration of the particle." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269116 1072 #uuid "34946b97-4ced-5633-a0a1-ce21c9cda71b" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269116 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=i-6j,\\,v=4i+3j,\\,t=3,\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $a,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 4i+3j & =(i-6j)+a(3) \\\\ 3i+9j & =3a \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{3}(3i+9j) \\\\  & =(i+3j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268715 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269117 1069 "q-136374" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269117 1071 "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $(2i+3j)\\,\\text{in}$ with a constant velocity of $-j\\,\\text{in/s}.$ Find the position vector of the particle after $4$ seconds." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269117 1072 #uuid "d3b4b03c-7469-51a0-ad96-778efdec2cf0" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269117 1074 "If a particle moves with constant velocity $u,$  the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut,\n$$\n\n \nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=2i+3j,\\,u=-j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=4\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & ={r}_{0}+ut \\\\  & =(2i+3j)+(-j)(4) \\\\  & =(2i+3j)-4j \\\\  & =(2i-j)\\,\\text{in}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268432 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269118 1069 "q-136376" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269118 1071 "A particle moves with constant acceleration. When $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle is $(5i+j)\\,\\text{ft/s},$ and when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle is $(5i+13j)\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ Calculate the acceleration of the particle." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269118 1072 #uuid "dd7931b3-4fa4-5b6b-83b3-c34ae9f02710" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269118 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=5i+j,\\,v=5i+13j,\\,t=4,\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $a,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 5i+13j & =(5i+j)+a(4) \\\\ 12j & =4a \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{4}(12j) \\\\  & =3j\\,{\\text{ft/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268802 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269119 1069 "q-136379" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269119 1071 "A particle moves from the origin with a constant velocity of  $(2i+5j)\\,\\text{cm/s}.$ Find the position vector of the particle after $3$ seconds." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269119 1072 #uuid "1975097d-dc96-5855-b219-e47df175c3c6" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269119 1074 "If a particle moves with constant velocity $u,$  the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut,\n$$\n\n \nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=2i+5j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & ={r}_{0}+ut \\\\  & =(0i+0j)+(2i+5j)(3) \\\\  & =(6i+15j)\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268643 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269120 1069 "q-136383" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269120 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269120 1071 "A particle moves from the origin with a constant velocity of $-3i\\,\\text{cm/s}.$ Find the position vector of the particle after $5$ seconds." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269120 1072 #uuid "ffde339a-9ca6-5dff-99f8-f5cd9326bfd0" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269120 1074 "If a particle moves with constant velocity $u,$  the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut,\n$$\n\n \nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=-3i.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & ={r}_{0}+ut \\\\  & =(0i+0j)+(-3i)(5) \\\\  & =-15i\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268786 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269121 1069 "q-136400" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269121 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269121 1071 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(2i-4j)\\,{\\text{ft/s}}^{2}.$ The velocity of the particle is $(-4i+16j)\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $(8i-8j)\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269121 1072 #uuid "33d33a4e-0ae7-51a7-ae9d-4c52249ecd66" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269121 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=2i-4j,\\,u=-4i+16j,\\,v=8i-8j\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 8i-8j & =(-4i+16j)+(2i-4j)t \\\\ 12i-24j & =(2i-4j)t \\\\ 12i-24j & =2ti-4tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}12 & = & 2t \\\\ -24 & = & -4t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,t=6\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268502 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269122 1069 "q-136407" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269122 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269122 1071 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(-i+7j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ The velocity of the particle is $(2i-5j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $(3i+2j)\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269122 1072 #uuid "bd4413f1-a39e-5835-89f1-54af9a2b9e18" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269122 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=-i+7j,\\,u=2i-5j,\\,v=3i+2j\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can attempt to solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 3i+2j & =(2i-5j)+(-i+7j)t \\\\ i+7j & =(-i+7j)t \\\\ i+7j & =-ti+7tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=-t \\\\ 7=7t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has no solution." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268516 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269123 1069 "q-136416" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269123 1071 "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $-2j\\,\\text{ft}$ with an acceleration of $4i\\,{\\text{ft/min}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(3i+j)\\,\\text{ft/min}.$ Find the position vector of the particle after $2$ minutes." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269123 1072 #uuid "a7bf5c71-d863-573a-99bf-be4eff6cad18" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269123 1074 "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=-2j,\\,u=3i+j,\\,a=4i.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=2\\,\\text{min}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(-2j)+(3i+j)(2)+\\frac{1}{2}(4i)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =-2j+(6i+2j)+2(4i) \\\\  & =6i+8i \\\\  & =14i\\,\\text{ft}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268533 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269124 1069 "q-136420" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269124 1071 "A particle moves from the origin with an acceleration of $(2i-4j)\\,{\\text{in/s}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(i-2j)\\,\\text{in/s}.$ Find the position vector of the particle after $3$ seconds." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269124 1072 #uuid "fa518f5e-9dd1-5e3a-95bd-478fb5aa0ef0" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269124 1074 "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=i-2j,\\,a=2i-4j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(0i+0j)+(i-2j)(3)+\\frac{1}{2}(2i-4j)(3)^{2} \\\\  & =(3i-6j)+9(i-2j) \\\\  & =(3i-6j)+(9i-18j) \\\\  & =(12i-24j)\\,\\text{in}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268588 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269125 1069 "q-136421" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269125 1071 "A particle moves from the origin with an acceleration of $(-i+3j)\\,{\\text{cm/s}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(4i+j)\\,\\text{cm/s}.$ Find the position vector of the particle after $2$ seconds." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269125 1072 #uuid "cb91ba67-e248-5be0-af85-0e04c256f2a1" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269125 1074 "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=4i+j,\\,a=-i+3j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(0i+0j)+(4i+j)(2)+\\frac{1}{2}(-i+3j)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =(8i+2j)+2(-i+3j) \\\\  & =(8i+2j)+(-2i+6j) \\\\  & =(6i+8j)\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268622 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269126 1069 "q-136427" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269126 1071 "A particle moves from the origin with an acceleration of $6i\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $-5j\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Find the position vector of the particle after $3$ hours." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269126 1072 #uuid "26f76050-6a6e-5056-957d-32427c67cc0f" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269126 1074 "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=-5j,\\,a=6i.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=3\\,\\text{h}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(0i+0j)+(-5j)(3)+\\frac{1}{2}(6i)(3)^{2} \\\\  & =-15j+27i \\\\  & =(27i-15j)\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268604 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269127 1069 "q-136435" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269127 1071 "A particle moves from the origin with a constant velocity of $(i-2j)\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Find the distance from the particle to the origin after $5$ hours." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269127 1072 #uuid "ea80603a-0aff-5a47-8726-2dc507337471" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269127 1074 "If a particle moves with constant velocity $u,$  the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=i-2j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=5\\,\\text{h}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & ={r}_{0}+ut \\\\  & =(0i+0j)+(i-2j)(5) \\\\  & =(5i-10j)\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the particle to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| & =|5i-10j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-10)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268441 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269128 1069 "q-136436" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269128 1071 "A particle moves from the origin with a constant velocity of $(3i+j)\\,\\text{cm/s}.$ Find the distance from the particle to the origin after $4$ seconds." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269128 1072 #uuid "c0373990-81b1-5759-b8f3-2379dfbc6c6e" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269128 1074 "If a particle moves with constant velocity $u,$  the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=3i+j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=4\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & ={r}_{0}+ut \\\\  & =(0i+0j)+(3i+j)(4) \\\\  & =(12i+4j)\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the particle to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| & =|12i+4j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268611 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269129 1069 "q-136554" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269129 1071 "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡a=-3,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a)=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269129 1072 #uuid "79094350-4856-50b1-9b17-ec0f97c230e4" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269129 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡a \\\\  & =-(-3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268792 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269130 1069 "q-136559" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269130 1071 "If $cot⁡x=\\frac{1}{4},$ then $4cot⁡(-x+4\\pi )+1=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269130 1072 #uuid "25442a2f-79df-59e1-a659-8bc32badc551" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269130 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x+4\\pi ) & =cot⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 4) \\\\  & =cot⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x) & =-cot⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4cot⁡(-x+4\\pi )+1 & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})+1 \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268745 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269131 1069 "q-136588" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269131 1071 "If $cot⁡a=-9,$ then $cot⁡(-a)=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269131 1072 #uuid "2fe46fba-1d41-5471-9453-bf3b903ae99c" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269131 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-a) & =-cot⁡a \\\\  & =-(-9) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268755 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269132 1069 "q-136623" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269132 1071 "If $sec⁡x=-2,$ then $5sec⁡(-x+12\\pi )+1=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269132 1072 #uuid "06c3c5c0-f8f8-5cfc-a337-ba456adc0f37" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269132 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $sec⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡(-x)=sec⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x+12\\pi ) & =sec⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 6) \\\\  & =sec⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x) & =sec⁡x \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5sec⁡(-x+12\\pi )+1 & =5\\cdot (-2)+1 \\\\  & =-10+1 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268541 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269133 1069 "q-136625" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269133 1071 "If $sec⁡x=-10,$ then $sec⁡(-x+4\\pi )=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269133 1072 #uuid "22d66223-2ab5-5bd5-a6f4-cbd5d5a40689" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269133 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $sec⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡(-x)=sec⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x+4\\pi ) & =sec⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =sec⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x) & =sec⁡x \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sec⁡(-x+4\\pi )=-10.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268719 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269134 1069 "q-136627" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269134 1070 #edb/ref 175