\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n7a+\\frac{b}{c}=7\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{7})+\\frac{2}{1}=0.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268563 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269148 1069 "q-13732" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269148 1071 "$\\int 5{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269148 1072 #uuid "94671e02-a8dc-5c87-b443-151869fd52de" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269148 1074 "If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{3x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268746 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269149 1069 "q-13760" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269149 1071 "Which of the following is an upper triangular matrix?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269149 1072 #uuid "3f081148-4183-50c8-903b-3014f5de1d45" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269149 1074 "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries *below* the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHence, it is an upper triangular matrix. \n\nBy contrast, matrices $B$, $C$, $D$, and $E$ each have at least one non-zero entry below the diagonal:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -