 the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 & 60 \\\\ 30 & 20 & 40\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 & 60 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{3})}{1}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268740 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269164 1069 "q-137926" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269164 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269164 1072 #uuid "f530dfca-e960-58b4-85b6-e3859f215c3f" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269164 1074 "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {3}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 \\\\ -8 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}25 & 50 \\\\ 50 & 100\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}50 \\\\ 100\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & 50 & 50 \\\\ 50 & 100 & 100\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}25 & 50 & 50 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=2-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=2+\\frac{(-2)}{1}=0.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268653 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269165 1069 "q-138018" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269165 1071 "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269165 1072 #uuid "63de7419-e75c-59e3-b8fc-e56c9267f133" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269165 1074 "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n2{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\