69183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268784 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269183 1069 "q-13817" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269183 1071 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ about $x=1$ is" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269183 1072 #uuid "682db2a3-d3d2-54b6-8a78-578de1c6ac8a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269183 1074 "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=1,$ we plug $a=1$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(1)(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(1),$ ${f}^{′}(1),$ and ${f}^{″}(1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}} & ⟹ &  & f(1) & ={e}^{-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\,2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(1) & =-2{e}^{-1} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4{x}^{2}-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(1) & =2{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(1),$ ${f}^{′}(1),$ and ${f}^{″}(1)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & ={e}^{-1}+(-2{e}^{-1})\\cdot (x-1)+(2{e}^{-1})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}-2{e}^{-1}(x-1)+{e}^{-1}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268629 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269184 1069 "q-13835" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269184 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{n}}=(x-1)^{3}+\\frac{(x-1)^{6}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(x-1)^{9}}{{3}^{3}}+⋯\\,.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269184 1072 #uuid "15de46ea-5bb2-5f10-a945-05b14689fc18" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269184 1074 "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{n}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{3(n+1)}}{(n+1)^{n+1}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{n}}{(x-1)^{3n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{(n+1)}\\cdot {(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{n}| \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)}\\cdot {(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{n} \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}\\cdot 0\\cdot {e}^{-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268567 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269185 1069 "q-13837" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269185 1071 "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269185 1072 #uuid "2ee2e00a-8c3c-5799-9d71-e6ddab6853db" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269185 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\frac{{u}^{2}}{3}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268566 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269186 1069 "q-13841" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269186 1071 "What is the value of ${\\int }_{e/2}^{{e}^{2}/2}\\frac{2}{3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269186 1072 #uuid "71f1a3c4-8374-541a-b0a7-9306ac0ecfa6" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269186 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2x}\\cdot 2=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e/2$ | ${e}^{2}/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e/2}^{{e}^{2}/2}\\frac{2}{3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{e/2}^{{e}^{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268507 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269187 1069 "q-13842" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269187 1071 "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269187 1072 #uuid "386635f7-97fa-5094-9755-51271e1af076" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269187 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x).$ Then  we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $e$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4e$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4e}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4e} \\\\  & ={\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {u}^{2}|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4e} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4e)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268707 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269188 1069 "q-13845" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269188 1071 "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{e/4}\\frac{1}{4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269188 1072 #uuid "ed857a50-0096-5fa3-bea2-e4ec1bd8cccb" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269188 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $e/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e/4}\\frac{1}{4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{e/4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268452 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269189 1069 "q-1388" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269189 1071 "Given that $b=⟨-1,7,4⟩$, find $-2b.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269189 1072 #uuid "48777d96-5d1a-5333-baf8-b41a8af70bcf" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269189 1074 "To compute the product, we just multiply each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2b & =(-2)\\cdot ⟨-1,7,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-2)\\cdot (-1),(-2)\\cdot 7,(-2)\\cdot 4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-14,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268854 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269190 1069 "q-138881" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269190 1071 "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2t$ meters per second.\nFind an expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269190 1072 #uuid "03bfb990-1f26-5661-91b6-3c45e83b54ef" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269190 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2t) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268552 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269191 1069 "q-138882" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269191 1071 "A body of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}$ meters per second.\nFind an expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269191 1072 #uuid "1839c4d0-2507-5658-b136-bf4255e48cce" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269191 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =6\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-2t) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268783 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269192 1069 "q-138889" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269192 1071 "A body of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=4\\sqrt{t}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269192 1072 #uuid "02a00c93-382a-5e86-9629-662a2b661d06" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269192 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{5}{t-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4}}-\\frac{5}{4-3} \\\\  & =1-5 \\\\  & =-4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F|=|-32|=32\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268816 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269193 1069 "q-138900" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269193 1071 "A body of mass $1.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{4}-{t}^{3}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269193 1072 #uuid "9f7aa7b0-ac31-5117-9ffe-fef9bf62cd13" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269193 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot (12{t}^{2}-6t) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268789 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269194 1069 "q-138901" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269194 1071 "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269194 1072 #uuid "9e7d45cf-09f2-50ec-8c0d-cf33253cd459" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269194 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (4{e}^{2t}-6t) \\\\  & =12{e}^{2t}-18t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268592 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269195 1069 "q-138902" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269195 1071 "A body of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=t-4\\sqrt{t}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269195 1072 #uuid "82349ab0-48c7-53d1-acea-6398c2dc41bd" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269195 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4\\sqrt{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4{t}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =1-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {t}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =1-2{t}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2{t}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {t}^{-3/2} \\\\  & ={t}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{t\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268821 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269196 1069 "q-138910" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269196 1071 "A body of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{2}-16t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269196 1072 #uuid "a1d91aed-e74e-5f69-8b3f-717dfe2eedd1" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269196 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-16t+5) \\\\  & =4t-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-16 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =16 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268855 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269197 1069 "q-138911" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269197 1071 "A body of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-24t+8$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269197 1072 #uuid "060dcb6c-1cca-56c6-a412-40178ff6c26e" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269197 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-24t+8) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-6t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-6t-24 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t-8 & =0 \\\\ (t+2)(t-4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268731 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269198 1069 "q-138912" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269198 1071 "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=6{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}-36t+2$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269198 1072 #uuid "29d26542-2f18-53b4-83de-dd8ee7531587" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269198 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}-36t+2) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-18t-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18{t}^{2}-18t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-2 & =0 \\\\ (t+1)(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268806 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269199 1069 "q-138913" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269199 1071 "A body of mass $9\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=(t-4){e}^{-t}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269199 1072 #uuid "97081d70-71f2-57bc-b330-ba202033fd6a" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269199 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-4){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}+(t-4)(-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(5-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-t){e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ 5-t & =0 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268538 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269200 1069 "q-138916" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269200 1071 "A body of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{4}-150{t}^{2}+8$ meters.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269200 1072 #uuid "eb76d076-2892-598b-932c-7f56dc7516a7" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269200 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-150{t}^{2}+8) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-300t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-300t) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-300\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{t}^{2}-300 & =0 \\\\ 12{t}^{2} & =300 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =25 \\\\ t & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268636 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269201 1069 "q-138968" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269201 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+1,\\,y={t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269201 1072 #uuid "f61336c1-ae50-5d39-92f7-f30e180cdcc2" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269201 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}(4+9{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(4+9{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\cdot |t|\\cdot \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\cdot t\\cdot \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268601 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269202 1069 "q-138979" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269202 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}-t,\\,y=4{e}^{t/2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269202 1072 #uuid "17c2517d-ea91-514b-ace4-ddd9a79ca203" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269202 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}-t)={e}^{t}-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{t/2})=2{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{({e}^{t}-1)}^{2}+{(2{e}^{t/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{t}+1+4{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression ${e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{({e}^{t}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\cdot |{e}^{t}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\cdot ({e}^{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-t{e}^{t}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ using integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⇒ & v={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x & =t{e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}-{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi [{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t] \\\\  & =2\\pi [{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})|}_{0}^{1}-{(t{e}^{t}-{e}^{t})|}_{0}^{1}] \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+e-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{2}+1-0)-(e-e)+(0-1)] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+e-3] \\\\  & =({e}^{2}+2e-6)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268506 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269203 1069 "q-139013" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269203 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=2{t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt{3}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269203 1072 #uuid "47912027-a927-58fe-87e4-6e2c0ef1bce2" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269203 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})=6t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(6t)}^{2}+{(6{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $36{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4},$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t] \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\cdot |6t|\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\cdot 6t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{t}^{2}$ Then, ${t}^{2}=u-1$ and $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(u-1){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{2{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =18\\pi [(\\frac{2({4}^{5/2})}{5}-\\frac{2({4}^{3/2})}{3})-(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3})] \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot [(\\frac{64}{5}-\\frac{16}{3})-(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot (\\frac{62}{5}-\\frac{14}{3}) \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot \\frac{116}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{696\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268673 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269204 1069 "q-139025" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269204 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=t+\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\in [1,e].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269204 1072 #uuid "92033a8c-e070-5bf1-96cf-1791e3001bbe" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269204 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=1$ to ${t}_{2}=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{2}{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\frac{1}{t})=1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{t})}^{2}+{(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $1+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}},$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{{(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot |1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot (1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+t+\\frac{2}{t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+t+\\frac{2}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}+2)-(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+0)] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2{e}^{2}}+2] \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{{e}^{2}}+4)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268831 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269205 1069 "q-139027" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [175921862