nsformations have been completed: \n\n$$\nB={A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{b}_{31}+{b}_{41}+{b}_{42}=4+0=4.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268814 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269223 1069 "q-13939" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269223 1071 "![](images/55/555cdb44522fab12ad67b54042ee4e47a085802df65c2b32a512eace16b92dd4.png)\n\nCalculate the area of the parallelogram in the figure." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269223 1072 #uuid "2faf3390-54b5-574c-8816-982dbf7402ae" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269223 1074 "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨1,2⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-5\\,|=5\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268439 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269224 1069 "q-139436" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269224 1071 "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\n- $R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]x$ is negative-definite\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269224 1072 #uuid "44381bf9-c027-58df-ba36-2d3bed72d50c" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269224 1074 "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{1}) & =1\\cdot 1=1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{2}) & =1\\cdot (-1)=-1<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268724 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269225 1069 "q-13944" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269225 1071 "![](images/7a/7a70285258e584708a7296a2a008536dec9c97750b48e5fe0eb2f8cb7761bed1.png)\n\nCalculate the area of the parallelogram in the figure." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269225 1072 #uuid "cad6730b-4b4a-5d91-acf2-b03ce7ce4d8e" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269225 1074 "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨3,2⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-10\\,|=10\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268573 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269226 1069 "q-139448" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269226 1071 "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-5I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,(A-5I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269226 1072 #uuid "928a6016-d7ba-559e-8b6b-42abd1307642" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269226 1074 "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-5I)^{3}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268466 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269227 1069 "q-139455" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269227 1071 "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269227 1072 #uuid "30844de8-e636-5e8f-ba19-823c3f8b1cf5" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269227 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative semi-definite since all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ is positive).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268468 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269228 1069 "q-139457" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269228 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is indefinite\n- $A$ is negative semi-definite" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269228 1072 #uuid "e96bcaa6-6221-5f7c-9daa-4047225c2c05" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269228 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, by computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since both eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive semi-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268523 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269229 1069 "q-139458" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269229 1071 "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive semi-definite? \n\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=10$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269229 1072 #uuid "80bdad61-ebf7-546e-ab4f-0e8486894231" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269229 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-negative.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is negative).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268818 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269230 1069 "q-139460" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269230 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive semi-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269230 1072 #uuid "36a88a67-992a-5e00-85ac-8bb48fc32f6c" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269230 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268575 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269231 1069 "q-139462" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269231 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269231 1072 #uuid "906865b3-32d2-5d7f-b7fc-d38ff3d60bed" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269231 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and  III are false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, neither the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ nor the matrix $A$ is positive-definite or negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268420 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269232 1069 "q-139468" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269232 1071 "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269232 1072 #uuid "ab64c662-c4ef-5a60-91d5-6874eab93cf6" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269232 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-17 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-17 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(5-3)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268692 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269233 1069 "q-139475" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269233 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269233 1072 #uuid "0761cfb5-82f2-5ff1-afcd-0792aa3de132" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269233 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}14 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{6}\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268790 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269234 1069 "q-139477" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269234 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269234 1072 #uuid "1a3f9a9b-9c5c-5941-8f74-a42675358e73" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269234 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268782 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269235 1069 "q-139478" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269235 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269235 1072 #uuid "12bd1922-22fd-5b46-aa73-11894915ba39" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269235 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 27\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}27 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{(-6)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{54}\\sqrt{54}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268457 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269236 1069 "q-139492" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269236 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $12,$ $9,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269236 1072 #uuid "e48008ac-4acd-5d33-8b94-d523fa814086" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269236 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=12\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $2\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268569 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269237 1069 "q-1395" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269237 1071 "The vector $u$ has magnitude $8$, the vector $v$ has magnitude $2,$ the angle between $u$ and $v$ is ${100}^{∘}$ and $n$ is the unit vector that points in the direction of $u\\times v.$ Find $u\\times v$ in terms of $n.$ Round your final answer to one decimal place where appropriate." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269237 1072 #uuid "42e7016d-2608-56a7-b446-4526679fb1db" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269237 1074 "We use the formula for the cross product and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|u|\\cdot |v|\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot n \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{100}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\  & =16\\cdot (0.984\\,80…)\\cdot n \\\\  & =(15.756\\,92…)\\cdot n \\\\  & ≈15.8n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268787 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269238 1069 "q-13951" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269238 1071 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269238 1072 #uuid "19e02ca3-a6f7-5909-838c-8df1ebb7ea40" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269238 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-5\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,23\\,|=23\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268630 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269239 1069 "q-139513" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269239 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269239 1072 #uuid "27ff07db-fdfd-5460-9a6e-b2c28eedff32" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269239 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}11 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{24}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{9}\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}})≈{24.09}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268835 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269240 1069 "q-139514" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269240 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269240 1072 #uuid "a21d579a-3fab-5e90-bfa1-e3d7308d1aaf" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269240 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{54}\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{9\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})≈{19.47}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268488 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269241 1069 "q-139516" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269241 1071 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269241 1072 #uuid "733dc56e-7cef-5043-973a-1d2c8fd2f794" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269241 1074 "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get $3{x}_{1}=4{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 25\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268711 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269242 1069 "q-139526" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269242 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $15,$ $10,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269242 1072 #uuid "72c2ac9a-8e8a-51d7-84bb-f51632a65f59" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269242 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $15,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=15\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{15}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{15}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268691 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269243 1069 "q-139528" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269243 1071 "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $6$ and $2,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269243 1072 #uuid "159dc54f-7305-519d-96d2-b73981f0f021" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269243 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $6,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=6\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{6}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268417 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269244 1069 "q-13953" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269244 1071 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269244 1072 #uuid "725abed1-dcc0-5071-8bbf-bf93146b75d3" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269244 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,6\\,|=6\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268644 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269245 1069 "q-139530" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true