 from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269251 1072 #uuid "bfc4c8a3-4cfa-58b3-bd85-7a7059d385d8" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269251 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268640 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269252 1069 "q-139555" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269252 1071 "![](images/2d/2d1cefc0c789518d21279c5c49a18f80220a2aad80973f0e84fda0ddaf25493b.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $100$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269252 1072 #uuid "5f621452-f60c-5e1d-b4df-620aa05fd780" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269252 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=10={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={10}^{2}=100,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=8={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={8}^{2}=64.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=100$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{1}.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268561 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269253 1069 "q-139571" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269253 1071 "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $6$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269253 1072 #uuid "3146337e-ee00-5ce0-a7e4-6d705552db4e" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269253 1074 "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $6$ | $10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268593 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269254 1069 "q-13958" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269254 1071 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=⟨0,2⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨3,3⟩.$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269254 1072 #uuid "1cdc9189-fe4f-5385-a547-6d74f5ba4b18" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269254 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-6\\,|=6\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268798 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269255 1069 "q-139593" 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533401 17592186045585 true] [17592186269255 1071 "| $x$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $5$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{